window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-ratios","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Accounting Ratios · Practice","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Z Ltd. has the following figures:\n50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each: Rs. 5,00,000\n10% Preference share capital: Rs. 2,00,000\nGeneral reserve: Rs. 1,50,000\nNet profit after tax: Rs. 1,20,000\nMarket price per equity share: Rs. 25\nWhich of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Earnings per share is Rs. 2.40","Book value per share is Rs. 17.00","Price earning ratio is 12.5 times","Return on shareholders' funds is 18.46%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Preference dividend = 10% of 2,00,000 = Rs. 20,000. Profit available to equity shareholders = 1,20,000 − 20,000 = Rs. 1,00,000. \n(1) Incorrect: EPS = 1,00,000/50,000 = Rs. 2, not Rs. 2.40. \n(2) Incorrect: Equity shareholders' funds = 8,50,000 − 2,00,000 = 6,50,000, so book value per share = Rs. 13. \n(3) Correct: P/E = 25/2 = 12.5 times. \n(4) Incorrect: Return on shareholders' funds = 1,20,000/8,50,000 = 14.12%. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about solvency ratios is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["When both are based on capital employed, the debt to capital employed ratio and the proprietary ratio add up to 1.","The interest coverage ratio is calculated by dividing net profit after interest and tax by interest on long-term debts.","When total assets to debt ratio is computed using net assets, it is the reciprocal of the debt to capital employed ratio.","A high debt-equity ratio may benefit owners through trading on equity if the rate of earnings on capital employed exceeds the rate of interest."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: for example, 0.25 + 0.75 = 1, as in NCERT Illustration 7.\n(2) Incorrect: the numerator is net profit BEFORE interest and tax. This is the profit available to pay interest.\n(3) Correct: net assets/long-term debt is the reciprocal of long-term debt/net assets.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states that trading on equity raises owners' returns when the return on capital employed exceeds the interest rate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Sunrise Ltd. is Rs. 3,60,000 and it earns a gross profit of 20% on cost. Opening inventory is Rs. 40,000 and closing inventory is Rs. 50,000. Calculate the inventory turnover ratio.","image":"","options":["6.4 times","8 times","6 times","6.67 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"When GP is 20% on cost, revenue = 120% of cost.\nCOGS = 3,60,000 × 100/120 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nAverage inventory = (40,000 + 50,000) ÷ 2 = Rs. 45,000.\nITR = 3,00,000 ÷ 45,000 = 6.67 times.\nTaking GP as 20% on sales instead gives COGS of Rs. 2,88,000 and the wrong answer of 6.4 times.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"While computing Return on Investment (Return on Capital Employed), which profit figure is taken as the numerator?","image":"","options":["Profit after Tax","Profit before Tax","Profit before Interest and Tax","Profit after Tax less Preference Dividend"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Capital employed includes shareholders' funds, debentures and long-term loans. The profit must therefore be the return earned for all these providers of long-term funds.\nInterest is the return to the lenders, so it is not deducted. Tax is also excluded, so PBIT is used.\nProfit after Tax is used for Return on Shareholders' Funds, not for ROI.\nProfit after Tax less Preference Dividend is used for EPS.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nAssertion (A): Quick ratio is considered a better measure of the liquidity position of a business than current ratio.\nReason (R): Quick ratio leaves out inventories and other current assets such as prepaid expenses and advance tax, which cannot be quickly converted into cash.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT treats the quick (acid-test) ratio as a better measure of liquidity than the current ratio. It is used as a supplementary check on liquidity.\nR is true. Quick assets are current assets minus inventories, prepaid expenses, advance tax and similar items.\nR explains A. The ratio is better precisely because these non-liquid current assets are excluded.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A very high inventory turnover ratio is generally good, but it should be interpreted carefully.\nReason (R): A high inventory turnover may result from buying goods in small lots or from selling goods quickly at a low margin to realise cash.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says a high inventory turnover is good, but it must be carefully interpreted.\nR is true. NCERT notes that a high turnover may be due to buying in small lots or selling quickly at a low margin to realise cash.\nR gives the very reason why a high ratio needs caution, so R correctly explains A.\nBy contrast, a low turnover is a danger signal. It may arise from bad buying or obsolete inventory.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): From the owners' point of view, a relatively higher debt-equity ratio may be beneficial.\nReason (R): Trading on equity raises the return to owners when the rate of earnings on capital employed is higher than the rate of interest payable on debt.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that greater use of debt can ensure higher returns for owners, even though lenders prefer a low ratio.\nR is true: this gain is called trading on equity. It works only when the firm earns more on capital employed than it pays as interest.\nR is the very reason a higher ratio can benefit owners, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"As per NCERT, a ratio is termed an 'accounting ratio' when it is:","image":"","options":["Any mathematical relationship between two numbers taken from any source","Calculated by relating two accounting numbers derived from the financial statements","Calculated only as a percentage of revenue from operations","Prepared by the auditor to certify the accounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A ratio is a number that shows the relationship between two or more numbers.\nWhen both numbers are accounting figures taken from the financial statements, the ratio is called an accounting ratio.\nOption (1) is too broad because it covers figures from any source.\nOption (3) is too narrow because accounting ratios can also be expressed as a fraction, a proportion or a number of times.\nOption (4) is wrong because preparing ratios is not an audit function.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Profit before interest and tax is used as the numerator while computing Return on Investment.\nReason (R): Capital employed includes debentures and long-term loans, and the interest on them is paid out of the profit earned on the total capital employed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that ROI = PBIT / Capital employed × 100.\nR is true: Capital employed means long-term funds, that is, shareholders' funds plus debentures and long-term loans.\nThe return on the whole capital employed must include the share earned for lenders, which is the interest. That is why profit is taken before interest, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the current ratio:\nInventories Rs. 60,000\nTrade receivables Rs. 40,000\nCash and cash equivalents Rs. 25,000\nPrepaid expenses Rs. 5,000\nTrade payables Rs. 40,000\nShort-term provisions Rs. 12,000","image":"","options":["2.5 : 1","2.4 : 1","3.25 : 1","2 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the current assets and current liabilities listed above.\nFormula: Current ratio = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities.\nCurrent assets = 60,000 + 40,000 + 25,000 + 5,000 = Rs. 1,30,000. Prepaid expenses are included.\nCurrent liabilities = 40,000 + 12,000 = Rs. 52,000.\nSo current ratio = 1,30,000 ÷ 52,000 = 2.5 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera Traders had revenue from operations of Rs. 6,00,000 during 2024-25. It sells goods at a gross profit of 25% on cost. The inventory turnover ratio is 6 times. Closing inventory was Rs. 20,000 more than opening inventory. What was the closing inventory?","image":"","options":["Rs. 90,000","Rs. 85,000","Rs. 70,000","Rs. 80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 6,00,000; GP = 25% on cost; inventory turnover = 6 times.\nCost of revenue from operations = 6,00,000 × 100/125 = Rs. 4,80,000.\nAverage inventory = 4,80,000 ÷ 6 = Rs. 80,000, so opening + closing = Rs. 1,60,000.\nLet opening = x; then closing = x + 20,000. So 2x + 20,000 = 1,60,000, giving x = Rs. 70,000.\nClosing inventory = Rs. 90,000.\nTaking 25% on sales instead of on cost wrongly gives Rs. 85,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Over ten years, Sunrise Ltd. bought no new fixed assets. Its fixed assets continue to appear at historical cost less depreciation. During this period, general prices roughly doubled, and the company's revenue from operations rose mainly because its selling prices increased. Its fixed assets turnover ratio rose from 2 times to 5 times, and the management claims a big improvement in efficiency.\nWhich limitation of ratio analysis most directly weakens this claim?","image":"","options":["Variations in accounting practices","Ignores price-level changes","Ratios based on unrelated figures","Lack of standardised definitions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Revenue is measured at current (inflated) prices, while fixed assets remain at old historical cost. The ratio therefore rises partly because of inflation, not because of better utilisation.\nAccounting assumes stable money value and ignores price-level changes, which makes comparisons across years misleading.\nOption (1) does not fit, because this is the same firm using the same practices. Option (3) does not fit, because revenue and fixed assets are meaningfully related.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the Debt-Equity Ratio:\nNon-current assets: Rs. 18,00,000\nCurrent assets: Rs. 6,00,000\nCurrent liabilities: Rs. 4,00,000\nNon-current liabilities (Long-term debts): Rs. 6,00,000","image":"","options":["0.33 : 1","0.30 : 1","0.25 : 1","0.43 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: NCA = 18,00,000; CA = 6,00,000; CL = 4,00,000; Long-term Debt = 6,00,000.\nFormula: Equity = Non-current assets + Working capital − Non-current liabilities.\nSubstituting: Working capital = 6,00,000 − 4,00,000 = 2,00,000. Equity = 18,00,000 + 2,00,000 − 6,00,000 = Rs. 14,00,000.\nSo Debt-Equity Ratio = 6,00,000 / 14,00,000 = 0.43 : 1. (0.30 : 1 wrongly uses capital employed as equity.)\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Its inventory is Rs. 60,000, and inventory is the only current asset that is not a quick asset. What are its current assets?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,50,000","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 1,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current ratio = 3 : 1, Quick ratio = 1.8 : 1, Inventory = Rs. 60,000. \nLet current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 3x and quick assets = 1.8x. \nInventory = Current assets − Quick assets, so 3x − 1.8x = 60,000. \nThis gives 1.2x = 60,000, so x = Rs. 50,000. \nSo current assets = 3 × 50,000 = Rs. 1,50,000. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following items is NOT included in current assets when calculating the current ratio?","image":"","options":["Advance tax","Prepaid expenses","Long-term loans and advances","Current investments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Current assets include current investments, inventories, trade receivables, cash and cash equivalents, short-term loans and advances, and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax and accrued income.\nAdvance tax and prepaid expenses are tempting choices because they are left out of quick assets. They are still current assets.\nLong-term loans and advances are non-current assets, so they are excluded.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has revenue from operations of ₹10,00,000. Its net profit before tax is ₹2,00,000 and the tax rate is 30%. Taking net profit as profit after tax, calculate the net profit ratio.","image":"","options":["20%","6%","30%","14%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = ₹10,00,000; PBT = ₹2,00,000; tax rate = 30%\nPAT = 2,00,000 − 30% of 2,00,000 = ₹1,40,000\nFormula: Net profit ratio = Net profit (PAT) / Revenue from operations × 100\nSubstituting: 1,40,000 / 10,00,000 × 100 = 14%\n20% is wrong because it uses profit before tax.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Situation) with List-II (Limitation):\nList-I\n(a) A firm's excellent employee morale and brand image are not reflected in any ratio\n(b) Net profit depends on the depreciation policy chosen by the accountant\n(c) Next year's performance is predicted only from past ratio trends\n(d) An Indian analyst cannot find industry averages to judge whether a ratio is satisfactory\nList-II\n(i) Lack of universally accepted standard levels\n(ii) Ignores qualitative aspects\n(iii) Forecasting\n(iv) Limitations of accounting data","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Morale and brand image are non-monetary, so ratios ignore them: (ii).\n(b) Profit is an opinion of the accountant based on the policies applied, which is a limitation of accounting data: (iv).\n(c) Forecasting from historical analysis alone is not feasible, because non-financial factors must also be considered: (iii).\n(d) There is no universal yardstick, and industry averages are not available in India: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 2 : 1. It pays a part of its trade payables (creditors) by cheque. What is the effect of this transaction on the current ratio?","image":"","options":["Current ratio will improve","Current ratio will reduce","Current ratio will not change","Current ratio will become exactly 1 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Paying creditors reduces current assets (bank) and current liabilities (trade payables) by the same amount.\nWhen a ratio is more than 1, an equal decrease in both terms raises the ratio.\nExample: CA Rs. 50,000 and CL Rs. 25,000. After paying Rs. 10,000, the ratio is 40,000/15,000 = 2.67 : 1, so it improves.\nIt would stay unchanged only if the ratio were exactly 1 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A ratio of purchases (Rs. 3,00,000) to furniture (Rs. 1,00,000), worked out as 3, has hardly any relevance for analysis.\nReason (R): A ratio must be calculated using numbers that are meaningfully correlated.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: purchases and furniture have no relationship with each other, so their ratio of 3 tells the analyst nothing.\nR is true: NCERT states that a ratio is useful only when the two numbers are meaningfully correlated.\nR explains A: the purchases-to-furniture ratio is useless precisely because the two figures are unrelated.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Its only non-quick current asset is inventory of Rs. 48,000. What is the working capital of the company?","image":"","options":["Rs. 80,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CR = 3 : 1, QR = 1.8 : 1, Inventory = Rs. 48,000.\nLet current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 3x and quick assets = 1.8x.\nInventory = Current assets – Quick assets, so 3x – 1.8x = 1.2x = 48,000. This gives x = Rs. 40,000.\nCurrent assets = 3 × 40,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nWorking capital = Current assets – Current liabilities = 1,20,000 – 40,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the current ratio:\nStatement I: If a firm's current ratio stays far below the normal level for long, the firm risks not paying its short-term debts on time, which may adversely affect its creditworthiness.\nStatement II: The higher the current ratio, the better the utilisation of the firm's resources.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says a low ratio endangers the business because short-term debts may not be paid on time. If the problem persists, creditworthiness suffers.\nStatement II is incorrect. A very high current ratio signals heavy investment in current assets, which means resources are under-utilised. The ratio should be reasonable, normally around 2 : 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The debt-equity ratio of Kiran Ltd. is 0.8 : 1. How many of the following transactions would REDUCE this ratio?\n(i) Issue of equity shares for cash\n(ii) Redemption of debentures in cash at par\n(iii) Conversion of debentures into equity shares\n(iv) Purchase of machinery by raising a long-term loan\n(v) Cash collected from trade receivables\n(vi) Issue of bonus shares by capitalising general reserve","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Reduces: equity rises while debt is unchanged.\n(ii) Reduces: long-term debt falls while equity is unchanged.\n(iii) Reduces: debt falls and equity rises at the same time.\n(iv) Increases: long-term debt rises.\n(v) No change: only the mix of current assets changes.\n(vi) No change: reserves move into share capital, so total shareholders' funds stay the same.\nThree transactions reduce the ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The Long-term Debts of a company are Rs. 7,00,000 and its Debt to Capital Employed Ratio is 0.35 : 1. Calculate its Debt-Equity Ratio.","image":"","options":["0.35 : 1","0.65 : 1","0.54 : 1","1.86 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Long-term Debt = Rs. 7,00,000; Debt to Capital Employed = 0.35.\nFind capital employed: Capital Employed = 7,00,000 / 0.35 = Rs. 20,00,000.\nFind shareholders' funds: Shareholders' Funds = 20,00,000 − 7,00,000 = Rs. 13,00,000. The Proprietary Ratio is therefore 0.65, since the two ratios add up to 1.\nDebt-Equity Ratio = 7,00,000 / 13,00,000 = 0.54 : 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Z Ltd. earned a Profit after Tax of Rs. 3,00,000. Its share capital consists of 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each and 10% Preference Share Capital of Rs. 5,00,000. What is its Earnings per Share?","image":"","options":["Rs. 6.00","Rs. 5.00","Rs. 4.00","Rs. 3.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = Rs. 3,00,000; 10% preference capital = Rs. 5,00,000; equity shares = 50,000.\nPreference dividend = 10% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nProfit available for equity shareholders = 3,00,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 2,50,000.\nFormula: EPS = Profit available for equity shareholders / Number of equity shares.\nSubstituting: 2,50,000 / 50,000 = Rs. 5.\nRs. 6 results from ignoring the preference dividend.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The following information relates to Sunrise Ltd. for the year 2024-25:\nProfit after tax: Rs. 3,00,000\n10% Preference share capital: Rs. 5,00,000\nEquity share capital: 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each\nMarket price of an equity share: Rs. 40\nCalculate the Price/Earning (P/E) ratio of the company.","image":"","options":["6.67 times","8 times","0.125 times","10 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = Rs. 3,00,000; 10% preference capital = Rs. 5,00,000; 50,000 equity shares; market price = Rs. 40.\nPreference dividend = 10% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nProfit available for equity shareholders = 3,00,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 2,50,000.\nEPS = 2,50,000 / 50,000 = Rs. 5.\nP/E ratio = Market price / EPS = 40 / 5 = 8 times.\nIf you forget to deduct the preference dividend, EPS becomes Rs. 6 and you get the wrong answer of 6.67.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Sunrise Traders, calculate the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio:\nTotal Revenue from Operations: Rs. 6,00,000\nCash Revenue from Operations: 25% of Total Revenue from Operations\nDebtors on 1 April: Rs. 50,000; Bills Receivable on 1 April: Rs. 20,000\nDebtors on 31 March: Rs. 55,000; Bills Receivable on 31 March: Rs. 25,000","image":"","options":["6 times","8 times","5.63 times","4.5 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total revenue = Rs. 6,00,000. Cash revenue = 25% = Rs. 1,50,000.\nCredit revenue = 6,00,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nOpening trade receivables = 50,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 70,000. Closing trade receivables = 55,000 + 25,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nAverage trade receivables = (70,000 + 80,000)/2 = Rs. 75,000.\nTrade Receivables Turnover = Net credit revenue / Average trade receivables = 4,50,000/75,000 = 6 times.\nOption (2) wrongly uses total revenue. Option (3) uses only closing receivables.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When both are computed on the basis of capital employed, the sum of the Debt to Capital Employed Ratio and the Proprietary Ratio of a company is equal to 1.\nStatement II: If the Total Assets to Debt Ratio is computed using net assets (capital employed) instead of total assets, it becomes the reciprocal of the Debt to Capital Employed Ratio.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Capital employed = Long-term debt + Shareholders' funds. So the debt share and the owners' share of capital employed add up to 1. For example, 0.25 + 0.75 = 1.\nStatement II is correct. Net assets to debt = Capital employed / Long-term debt. This is the reciprocal of Long-term debt / Capital employed, and NCERT notes this point directly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A rise in the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio is generally a favourable sign for the liquidity of a firm.\nReason (R): A higher trade receivables turnover means receivables are converted into cash more quickly, which shortens the average collection period.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that liquidity depends on the speed at which trade receivables are realised.\nR is true: a higher turnover means speedy collection. Since collection period = days / turnover, a higher ratio gives a shorter collection period.\nR directly explains why a higher ratio improves liquidity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A relatively high debt-equity ratio may be welcomed by the equity shareholders of a company even though lenders consider it risky.\nReason (R): When the rate of earnings on capital employed is higher than the rate of interest payable on debt, greater use of debt (trading on equity) raises the return to owners.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Lenders prefer a low ratio for security. Owners, however, may favour more debt.\nR is true. This is the idea of trading on equity: if the firm earns more on the borrowed funds than the interest it pays, the surplus goes to the shareholders.\nR gives the reason why owners welcome a higher ratio, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a company is 2 : 1. Consider the following transactions:\n(i) Payment to trade payables by cheque\n(ii) Purchase of goods on credit\n(iii) Sale of machinery (book value Rs. 50,000) for Rs. 40,000 in cash\n(iv) Sale of goods costing Rs. 10,000 for Rs. 12,000 in cash\n(v) Cash collected from debtors\nHow many of the above transactions will improve the current ratio?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Improves: an equal fall in current assets and current liabilities raises a ratio that is above 1.\n(ii) Reduces: an equal rise in both items lowers a ratio that is above 1.\n(iii) Improves: cash (a current asset) rises, and the loss on sale does not matter because machinery is a non-current asset.\n(iv) Improves: inventory falls by Rs. 10,000 while cash rises by Rs. 12,000.\n(v) No change: one current asset simply replaces another.\nSo three transactions improve the ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera Ltd. reports an average collection period of 73 days (take 365 days in a year). Its cash revenue from operations is 20% of total revenue from operations. Data on trade receivables:\nOn 1 April: Debtors Rs. 80,000 (no provision); Bills Receivable Rs. 20,000\nOn 31 March: Debtors shown in the balance sheet at Rs. 1,30,000 after deducting a provision for doubtful debts of Rs. 10,000; Bills Receivable Rs. 20,000\nWhat is the total revenue from operations of Meera Ltd.?","image":"","options":["Rs. 8,12,500","Rs. 6,50,000","Rs. 7,81,250","Rs. 7,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Trade receivables turnover = 365/73 = 5 times.\nOpening trade receivables = 80,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nClosing trade receivables, taking debtors before provision = (1,30,000 + 10,000) + 20,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nAverage trade receivables = (1,00,000 + 1,60,000)/2 = Rs. 1,30,000.\nCredit revenue = 5 × 1,30,000 = Rs. 6,50,000. This is 80% of total revenue.\nTotal revenue = 6,50,000/0.80 = Rs. 8,12,500.\nOption (3) uses net debtors. Option (2) stops at credit revenue.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following extract of Meera Traders Ltd., calculate the quick ratio:\nInventories Rs. 60,000\nTrade receivables Rs. 45,000\nCash and cash equivalents Rs. 25,000\nCurrent investments Rs. 20,000\nPrepaid expenses Rs. 6,000\nAdvance tax Rs. 4,000\nTrade payables Rs. 70,000\nShort-term provisions Rs. 10,000","image":"","options":["1.125 : 1","1.25 : 1","1.175 : 1","2 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the current assets and current liabilities listed above.\nFormula: Quick ratio = Quick assets ÷ Current liabilities. Quick assets = Current assets – (Inventories + Prepaid expenses + Advance tax).\nQuick assets = 45,000 + 25,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 90,000.\nCurrent liabilities = 70,000 + 10,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nSo quick ratio = 90,000 ÷ 80,000 = 1.125 : 1.\nIf only inventory is excluded, the result is 1.25 : 1, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A bank is considering a long-term loan to Meera Textiles Ltd. Its credit officer wants a single ratio that meets two conditions:\n(a) it should indicate the safety of the periodic interest payments on long-term debt, i.e., it must be a solvency ratio; and\n(b) both of its figures should come from the Statement of Profit and Loss, so the ratio can be computed even before the Balance Sheet is finalised.\nWhich ratio meets both requirements?","image":"","options":["Debt-equity ratio","Interest coverage ratio","Operating ratio","Total assets to debt ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Interest coverage ratio = net profit before interest and tax ÷ interest on long-term debts.\nIt is a solvency ratio that shows how many times interest is covered. Both of its figures come from the P&L, so it meets both conditions.\nDebt-equity and total assets to debt are solvency ratios, but they need Balance Sheet figures, so they fail condition (b).\nOperating ratio uses only P&L figures, but it is a profitability ratio, so it fails condition (a).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has Shareholders' Funds of Rs. 6,00,000 and Long-term Debts of Rs. 2,00,000. Calculate its Proprietary Ratio based on capital employed.","image":"","options":["0.25 : 1","0.75 : 1","3 : 1","1.33 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Shareholders' Funds = Rs. 6,00,000; Long-term Debts = Rs. 2,00,000.\nFormula: Proprietary Ratio = Shareholders' Funds / Capital Employed, where Capital Employed = Long-term Debts + Shareholders' Funds.\nSubstituting: Capital Employed = 2,00,000 + 6,00,000 = Rs. 8,00,000. Ratio = 6,00,000 / 8,00,000.\nSo Proprietary Ratio = 0.75 : 1. (0.25 : 1 is the Debt to Capital Employed Ratio.)\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: When the figures of opening and closing inventory are not available, the single inventory figure given is treated as average inventory for computing the inventory turnover ratio.\nStatement II: An inventory turnover ratio of 6 implies that inventory is converted into revenue from operations six times during the year.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct, Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect, Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When opening and closing inventory are not given separately, NCERT treats the given 'Inventory' as the average inventory.\nStatement II is correct. The ratio shows how many times inventory turns into revenue from operations in an accounting period, so 6 means six times a year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An inter-firm comparison based on ratios may be misleading if the two firms follow different methods of inventory valuation and depreciation.\nStatement II: Ratio analysis reflects the qualitative aspects of a business, such as employee morale and quality of management.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Variations in accounting practices (inventory valuation, depreciation, treatment of intangibles) are a stated limitation of ratio analysis. They make cross-sectional comparison doubtful.\nStatement II is false. Ratios are based on accounting data, which is monetary. Ratios therefore ignore qualitative, non-monetary aspects such as morale or management quality.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Credit revenue from operations of Sona Traders for 2023-24 was Rs. 6,00,000. Trade receivables were Rs. 70,000 on 1 April 2023 and Rs. 80,000 on 31 March 2024. Calculate the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio.","image":"","options":["8 times","7.5 times","8.57 times","4 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Credit revenue = Rs. 6,00,000. Opening TR = Rs. 70,000 and closing TR = Rs. 80,000.\nFormula: TR Turnover = Net credit revenue / Average trade receivables.\nAverage TR = (70,000 + 80,000)/2 = Rs. 75,000.\nSubstituting: 6,00,000 / 75,000 = 8.\nSo the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio = 8 times.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"One analyst treats 'liquid liabilities' as all current liabilities, while another treats them as current liabilities less bank overdraft. Which limitation of ratios does this illustrate?","image":"","options":["Ratios based on unrelated figures","Lack of standardised definitions","Ignores price-level changes","Lack of ability to resolve problems"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives this exact example: there is no standard definition of liquid liabilities.\nNormally the term includes all current liabilities, but sometimes it means current liabilities less bank overdraft.\nThe same ratio can therefore give different values depending on the definition used, which shows a lack of standardised definitions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Kavya Traders is Rs. 2,40,000 and its gross profit is Rs. 30,000. The gross profit ratio is:","image":"","options":["8%","14.29%","12.5%","20%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Gross profit = Rs. 30,000; Revenue from operations = Rs. 2,40,000\nFormula: Gross profit ratio = Gross profit / Revenue from operations × 100\nSubstituting: 30,000 / 2,40,000 × 100 = 12.5%\nThe distractor 14.29% comes from dividing by cost of revenue (Rs. 2,10,000), which is the wrong base.\nSo gross profit ratio = 12.5%\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has current assets of Rs. 1,50,000 and current liabilities of Rs. 60,000, giving a current ratio of 2.5 : 1. The management wants to raise the current ratio to 3 : 1 by paying off some trade payables in cash. What amount must be paid to the creditors?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: current assets Rs. 1,50,000, current liabilities Rs. 60,000, target ratio 3 : 1.\nPaying creditors an amount x reduces both current assets and current liabilities by x.\nCondition: (1,50,000 − x) ÷ (60,000 − x) = 3.\nSo 1,50,000 − x = 1,80,000 − 3x, which gives 2x = 30,000 and x = Rs. 15,000.\nCheck: 1,35,000 ÷ 45,000 = 3 : 1.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A low trade payables turnover ratio may indicate that the firm is delaying payments to its suppliers.\nReason (R): The lower the trade payables turnover ratio, the longer the average payment period.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that a lower trade payables turnover means the supplier allows credit for a long period, or that payment to suppliers is delayed.\nR is true. Average payment period = Days in a year / Trade payables turnover, so a lower ratio gives a longer payment period.\nR explains A: a long average payment period is exactly what signals possible delay in paying suppliers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Profit figure used in the numerator):\nList-I\nA. Return on Investment\nB. Return on Shareholders' Funds\nC. Earnings per Share\nD. Gross Profit Ratio\nList-II\n(i) Profit after tax\n(ii) Profit before interest and tax\n(iii) Profit after tax less preference dividend\n(iv) Revenue from operations less cost of revenue from operations","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(ii): ROI relates PBIT to capital employed.\nB-(i): Return on shareholders' funds (RONW) relates PAT to shareholders' funds.\nC-(iii): EPS uses profit available to equity shareholders, which is PAT minus preference dividend.\nD-(iv): Gross profit is revenue from operations minus cost of revenue from operations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Mohan Ltd. for 2023-24 was ₹5,00,000 and its cost of revenue from operations was ₹3,75,000. Calculate the gross profit ratio.","image":"","options":["33.33%","25%","75%","20%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = ₹5,00,000; Cost of revenue from operations = ₹3,75,000\nGross profit = ₹5,00,000 − ₹3,75,000 = ₹1,25,000\nFormula: Gross profit ratio = Gross profit / Revenue from operations × 100\nSubstituting: 1,25,000 / 5,00,000 × 100 = 25%\n33.33% wrongly divides gross profit by cost instead of by revenue.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A lower operating ratio is considered a healthy sign for a business.\nReason (R): Operating profit ratio is equal to 100 minus operating ratio, so a lower operating ratio leaves a larger portion of revenue from operations as operating profit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that a lower operating ratio is a very healthy sign, because it shows that operating costs are low compared with revenue.\nR is true. Operating Profit Ratio = 100 − Operating Ratio.\nR explains A. For example, if the operating ratio falls from 85% to 75%, the operating margin rises from 15% to 25%. This shows better operational efficiency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about inventory turnover ratio are correct?\n(i) It is calculated as cost of revenue from operations divided by average inventory.\n(ii) A high inventory turnover ratio is always a sign of efficiency and needs no further interpretation.\n(iii) It indicates how many times inventory is purchased or replaced during a year.\n(iv) It is also regarded as a measure of liquidity.\n(v) Carriage outwards is added while computing cost of revenue from operations.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the standard formula.\n(ii) Incorrect: a high ratio must be interpreted carefully. It may come from buying in small lots or selling at low margins.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT says it shows how many times inventory is replaced in a year.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT calls it a measure of liquidity as well.\n(v) Incorrect: carriage outwards is a selling expense, not part of cost of revenue.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Comparing the inventory turnover ratios of two companies in the same industry may be misleading if one values inventory by FIFO and the other by weighted average method.\nReason (R): Variations in accounting practices among enterprises make a valid cross-sectional comparison of their financial statements difficult.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Different inventory valuation methods produce different inventory and cost figures, so the turnover ratios are not comparable.\nR is true. NCERT states that differing policies for inventory valuation, depreciation and intangibles leave a big question mark on cross-sectional (inter-firm) analysis.\nR directly explains why the comparison in A is misleading.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following particulars of Sunrise Ltd., calculate the operating profit ratio:\nRevenue from operations: ₹8,00,000\nCost of revenue from operations: ₹5,00,000\nOffice and administrative expenses: ₹40,000\nSelling and distribution expenses: ₹60,000\nDepreciation: ₹20,000\nInterest on debentures: ₹30,000\nLoss on sale of machinery: ₹10,000\nDividend received: ₹5,000","image":"","options":["22.5%","17.5%","37.5%","21.25%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Operating cost includes cost of revenue, administrative expenses, selling expenses and depreciation. It excludes interest, loss on sale of assets and dividend received.\nOperating cost = 5,00,000 + 40,000 + 60,000 + 20,000 = ₹6,20,000\nOperating ratio = 6,20,000 / 8,00,000 × 100 = 77.5%\nOperating profit ratio = 100 − 77.5 = 22.5%\n17.5% is wrong because it wrongly includes interest and the loss on sale.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about the quick ratio.\nStatement I: Normally, a quick ratio of 1 : 1 is considered safe.\nStatement II: A very high quick ratio is always desirable, because it shows the maximum short-term safety without any disadvantage.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT advocates 1 : 1 as a safe quick ratio.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT warns that a high quick ratio suggests unnecessary deployment of resources in less profitable short-term investments. An unnecessarily low ratio, on the other hand, is very risky.\nSo the ratio should be reasonable, neither too high nor too low.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sona Traders Ltd. has a current ratio of 2.5 : 1 and working capital of Rs. 1,20,000. The finance manager believes a ratio close to the safe norm of 2 : 1 is sufficient. During the month, the company purchases goods worth Rs. 40,000 on credit. What will be the current ratio after this transaction?","image":"","options":["2.5 : 1","3 : 1","2 : 1","1.67 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let current liabilities = x, so current assets = 2.5x. Working capital = 2.5x − x = 1.5x = 1,20,000, which gives x = Rs. 80,000.\nSo current liabilities = Rs. 80,000 and current assets = Rs. 2,00,000.\nThe credit purchase increases inventory (a current asset) and trade payables (a current liability) by Rs. 40,000 each.\nNew ratio = 2,40,000 ÷ 1,20,000 = 2 : 1. Raising only current assets would wrongly give 3 : 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Kavya Ltd. reports working capital of Rs. 1,80,000. Its current ratio is 2.5 : 1 and its quick ratio is 1.2 : 1. Apart from quick assets, its current assets consist only of inventory and prepaid expenses of Rs. 12,000. Calculate the value of inventory.","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,44,000","Rs. 1,56,000","Rs. 1,68,000","Rs. 1,32,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Working capital = Rs. 1,80,000, CR = 2.5 : 1, QR = 1.2 : 1, Prepaid expenses = Rs. 12,000.\nWorking capital = CA – CL = 2.5CL – CL = 1.5CL = 1,80,000, so CL = Rs. 1,20,000.\nCurrent assets = 2.5 × 1,20,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nQuick assets = 1.2 × 1,20,000 = Rs. 1,44,000.\nNon-quick current assets = 3,00,000 – 1,44,000 = Rs. 1,56,000.\nSo inventory = 1,56,000 – 12,000 = Rs. 1,44,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"While computing the Operating Ratio of a company, which one of the following items will be included in operating expenses?","image":"","options":["Loss on sale of old machinery","Interest paid on debentures","Depreciation on office furniture","Loss of goods by fire"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Operating ratio = (Cost of Revenue from Operations + Operating Expenses) / Net Revenue from Operations × 100.\nAs per NCERT, operating expenses include office, administrative, selling and distribution expenses, depreciation and employee benefit expenses. So depreciation on office furniture is an operating expense.\nNon-operating items are excluded. Loss on sale of assets, interest paid and loss by fire are all non-operating.\nInterest on debentures is the most tempting choice, but it is a financial charge. It is not a cost of operations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are objectives of ratio analysis as stated in NCERT?\n(i) To know the areas of business that need more attention\n(ii) To provide information for cross-sectional analysis by comparing with the best industry standards\n(iii) To provide information useful for making projections and estimates for the future\n(iv) To remove the need for preparing financial statements\n(v) To measure qualitative factors such as employee morale","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: identifying areas that need attention is a stated objective.\n(ii) Correct: cross-sectional comparison with the best industry standards is a stated objective.\n(iii) Correct: providing information for projections and estimates is a stated objective.\n(iv) Incorrect: ratios are derived from financial statements, so they cannot replace them.\n(v) Incorrect: ratios ignore qualitative (non-monetary) aspects.\nSo three statements are objectives.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has quick assets of ₹1,20,000. Its current liabilities are ₹1,00,000, which include a bank overdraft of ₹20,000. Analyst P takes liquid liabilities as all current liabilities. Analyst Q takes liquid liabilities as current liabilities less bank overdraft. By how much does Q's quick ratio exceed P's quick ratio?","image":"","options":["0.30","0.20","0.25","0.50"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Quick assets = ₹1,20,000; Current liabilities = ₹1,00,000; Bank overdraft = ₹20,000.\nFormula: Quick ratio = Quick assets / Liquid liabilities.\nP: 1,20,000 / 1,00,000 = 1.20 : 1. Q: 1,20,000 / (1,00,000 − 20,000) = 1.50 : 1.\nSo the difference = 1.50 − 1.20 = 0.30. The same data gives different ratios because of the lack of standardised definitions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The accountant of Sharma Enterprises has the following data for 2024-25:\nRevenue from operations: Rs. 6,00,000\nGross profit: 25% on revenue from operations\nInventory Turnover Ratio: 6 times\nInventory at the end is Rs. 30,000 more than the inventory in the beginning.\nWhat is the value of the inventory at the end?","image":"","options":["Rs. 90,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 1,05,000","Rs. 95,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gross profit = 25% of 6,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nSo, cost of revenue from operations = 6,00,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nAverage inventory = Cost of revenue from operations ÷ ITR = 4,50,000/6 = Rs. 75,000.\nLet opening inventory be x. Then (x + x + 30,000)/2 = 75,000, so x + 15,000 = 75,000 and x = Rs. 60,000.\nClosing inventory = 60,000 + 30,000 = Rs. 90,000.\nRs. 60,000 is the opening inventory, not the closing inventory.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Formula):\nList-I\n(A) Debt-Equity Ratio\n(B) Debt to Capital Employed Ratio\n(C) Proprietary Ratio\n(D) Total Assets to Debt Ratio\nList-II\n(I) Shareholders' Funds / (Long-term Debts + Shareholders' Funds)\n(II) Total Assets / Long-term Debts\n(III) Long-term Debts / Shareholders' Funds\n(IV) Long-term Debts / (Long-term Debts + Shareholders' Funds)","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Debt-Equity = Long-term debts / Shareholders' funds, so A-III.\n(B) Debt to Capital Employed = Long-term debts / Capital employed, where capital employed = debt + equity. So B-IV.\n(C) Proprietary = Shareholders' funds / Capital employed, so C-I.\n(D) Total Assets to Debt = Total assets / Long-term debts, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Nirmal Ltd., calculate the Total Assets to Debt Ratio:\nEquity share capital: Rs. 6,00,000\nGeneral reserve: Rs. 1,00,000\n12% Debentures: Rs. 1,40,000\nTrade payables: Rs. 1,20,000\nOutstanding expenses: Rs. 40,000","image":"","options":["3.6 : 1","2.5 : 1","7.14 : 1","6 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets = Total of equity and liabilities = 6,00,000 + 1,00,000 + 1,40,000 + 1,20,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 10,00,000.\nLong-term debt = Debentures = Rs. 1,40,000. Trade payables and outstanding expenses are current liabilities, not long-term debt.\nFormula: Total Assets to Debt Ratio = Total assets / Long-term debts.\nSubstituting: 10,00,000 / 1,40,000 = 7.14 : 1.\nThe option 3.6 : 1 is wrong because it divides by total debts, which wrongly includes current liabilities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Activity Ratio) with List-II (Basis of Calculation):\nList-I\nA. Fixed Assets Turnover Ratio\nB. Working Capital Turnover Ratio\nC. Capital Employed Turnover Ratio\nD. Trade Payables Turnover Ratio\nList-II\n(i) Revenue from operations ÷ (Current assets − Current liabilities)\n(ii) Net credit purchases ÷ Average trade payables\n(iii) Net revenue from operations ÷ Net fixed assets\n(iv) Revenue from operations ÷ (Shareholders' funds + Long-term debts)","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Fixed assets turnover relates net revenue from operations to net fixed assets.\nB-(i): Working capital turnover uses working capital, which is current assets minus current liabilities.\nC-(iv): Capital employed (net assets) is shareholders' funds plus long-term debts.\nD-(ii): Trade payables arise from credit purchases, so this ratio uses net credit purchases over average trade payables.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, a current ratio within which of the following ranges is normally considered safe for a business?","image":"","options":["1 : 1","2 : 1","0.5 : 1","4 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says it is normally safe to have a current ratio within the range of 2 : 1.\n1 : 1 is the norm for the quick (acid-test) ratio, not the current ratio.\n0.5 : 1 is too low and risks default on short-term debts.\n4 : 1 is too high and suggests idle current assets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Interest coverage ratio is calculated using profit before interest and tax rather than profit after tax.\nReason (R): Interest on long-term debts is a charge against profit and is paid before tax is computed. So the whole of the profit before interest and tax is available to meet it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the numerator of ICR is net profit before interest and tax.\nR is true: interest is deducted before taxable profit is found.\nR also explains A. Profit after tax has already been reduced by interest and tax, so it would understate the profit available to pay interest. That is why PBIT is used.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Sharda Ltd. has issued 12% Debentures of Rs. 5,00,000. Its net profit before tax for the year is Rs. 2,40,000. Calculate the interest coverage ratio.","image":"","options":["4 times","6 times","5 times","3 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 12% Debentures = Rs. 5,00,000; NPBT = Rs. 2,40,000.\nInterest = 12% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nPBIT = NPBT + Interest = 2,40,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nICR = 3,00,000 ÷ 60,000 = 5 times.\nUsing NPBT directly (2,40,000 ÷ 60,000 = 4) is wrong because interest has already been deducted from that figure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following extract of the balance sheet of Meera Ltd., calculate the current ratio:\nInventories Rs. 60,000; Trade receivables Rs. 45,000; Prepaid expenses Rs. 5,000; Advance tax Rs. 10,000; Cash and cash equivalents Rs. 20,000; Current investments Rs. 20,000; Non-current investments Rs. 40,000; Trade payables Rs. 50,000; Short-term borrowings (bank overdraft) Rs. 20,000; Short-term provisions Rs. 10,000; Long-term borrowings Rs. 1,00,000","image":"","options":["2 : 1","1.81 : 1","2.67 : 1","2.5 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: Current ratio = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities.\nCurrent assets = 60,000 + 45,000 + 5,000 + 10,000 + 20,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 1,60,000. Non-current investments are excluded.\nCurrent liabilities = 50,000 + 20,000 + 10,000 = Rs. 80,000. Long-term borrowings are excluded.\nCurrent ratio = 1,60,000 ÷ 80,000 = 2 : 1.\nCommon errors: dropping prepaid expenses and advance tax gives 1.81 : 1, and leaving out the bank overdraft gives 2.67 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The debt-equity ratio of Sunrise Ltd. is 0.5 : 1. Which of the following transactions will DECREASE this ratio?","image":"","options":["Cash collected from trade receivables (debtors)","Redemption of debentures","Purchase of goods on credit","Sale of goods for cash at cost"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Debt-equity ratio = Long-term debts / Shareholders' funds.\n(1) Collection from debtors only changes the mix of current assets (cash rises, debtors fall). Neither debt nor equity changes, so the ratio is unchanged.\n(2) Redemption of debentures reduces long-term debt while equity stays the same, so the ratio falls. For example, ₹4,00,000/₹10,00,000 = 0.4 : 1 instead of 0.5 : 1.\n(3) A credit purchase affects only inventory and trade payables, which are current items. There is no change in the ratio.\n(4) A cash sale at cost only converts inventory into cash. There is no change in the ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ratio analysis ignores the qualitative (non-monetary) aspects of a business because accounting records only monetary information.\nStatement II: Since different enterprises may follow different methods of inventory valuation and depreciation, inter-firm comparison through ratios is always fully reliable.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Accounting captures only quantitative (monetary) facts, so ratios ignore qualitative factors. \nStatement II is incorrect. Variations in accounting practices are a limitation of ratio analysis. They put a big question mark on cross-sectional (inter-firm) comparison, so such comparison is not fully reliable. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Firm P has current assets of Rs. 4,00,000 and current liabilities of Rs. 80,000. Firm Q, in the same industry, has current assets of Rs. 1,20,000 and current liabilities of Rs. 1,50,000.\nWhich of the following interpretations is correct?","image":"","options":["Firm P is in the best position, because a higher current ratio is always better","Firm Q is safer than P, because its current liabilities exceed its current assets only slightly","Firm P may have idle funds locked in current assets, while Firm Q risks being unable to pay short-term debts on time, which may hurt its creditworthiness","Both firms are equally sound, because the current ratio ignores how resources are utilised"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Firm P: 4,00,000 ÷ 80,000 = 5 : 1, which is far above the safe level of about 2 : 1.\nSuch a very high ratio means heavy investment in current assets, i.e. under-utilised resources.\nFirm Q: 1,20,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = 0.8 : 1, which is very low.\nA low ratio risks default on short-term debt and, if it persists, harms creditworthiness.\nOption (1) is wrong because NCERT says the ratio should be neither very high nor very low.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A very high current ratio is not necessarily a good sign for a business.\nReason (R): A very high current ratio reflects heavy investment in current assets, indicating under-utilisation or improper utilisation of resources.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says the current ratio should be reasonable, neither very high nor very low.\nR is true. A very high ratio means heavy investment in current assets, which reflects under-utilisation or improper utilisation of resources.\nR gives the exact reason why a high ratio is not desirable, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Kavya Ltd. reported a Profit after Tax of Rs. 2,10,000 for the year. The tax rate is 30%. Its long-term funds consist of Shareholders' Funds of Rs. 15,00,000 and 12% Debentures of Rs. 5,00,000. Calculate its Return on Investment (Return on Capital Employed).","image":"","options":["10.5%","15%","18%","24%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = Rs. 2,10,000; tax rate = 30%.\nPBT = 2,10,000 × 100 / (100 − 30) = Rs. 3,00,000.\nInterest on debentures = 12% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nPBIT = 3,00,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 3,60,000.\nCapital employed = 15,00,000 + 5,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000.\nROI = 3,60,000 / 20,00,000 × 100 = 18%.\n24% wrongly divides PBIT by shareholders' funds alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: Liquidity ratios measure the firm's ability to pay amounts due as and when they fall due, and are essentially short-term in nature.\nStatement II: In practice, classifying ratios on the basis of the financial statements from which their figures are drawn is the most commonly used classification.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Liquidity means the ability to pay amounts due on time, and liquidity ratios are short-term in nature.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that classification by financial statements (the traditional classification) is rarely used in practice. The functional classification is the most commonly used.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The debt-equity ratio of a company is 0.5 : 1. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Further issue of equity shares will decrease the ratio","Redemption of debentures will decrease the ratio","Purchase of goods on credit will not change the ratio","Cash received from debtors will increase the ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: issuing equity shares raises shareholders' funds, so the ratio falls.\nOption (2) is correct: redeeming debentures lowers long-term debt, so the ratio falls.\nOption (3) is correct: buying goods on credit affects only current assets and current liabilities, not long-term debt or equity.\nOption (4) is incorrect: collecting cash from debtors only changes the mix of current assets. Neither debt nor equity changes, so the ratio is unchanged.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"M Ltd. has 1,00,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, 8% Preference Share Capital of Rs. 5,00,000 and Reserves and Surplus of Rs. 3,00,000. Its Profit after Tax is Rs. 2,40,000, and it declared a dividend of 12% on equity shares.\nConsider the following statements:\n(i) EPS is Rs. 2.40.\n(ii) Dividend payout ratio is 0.6.\n(iii) Book value per share is Rs. 18.\n(iv) If the market price is Rs. 30 per share, the P/E ratio is 15 times.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(ii) and (iv) only","(i) and (iii) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Preference dividend = 8% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 40,000.\nEPS = (2,40,000 − 40,000) / 1,00,000 = Rs. 2, so (i) is incorrect.\nDPS = 12% × Rs. 10 = Rs. 1.20, so payout = 1.20 / 2 = 0.6. (ii) is correct.\nEquity shareholders' funds = 10,00,000 + 3,00,000 = Rs. 13,00,000. Preference capital is excluded.\nBVPS = 13,00,000 / 1,00,000 = Rs. 13, so (iii) is incorrect.\nP/E = 30 / 2 = 15 times, so (iv) is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The trade receivables turnover ratio is a composite ratio because it uses one figure from the Statement of Profit and Loss and one from the Balance Sheet.\nStatement II: The gross profit ratio is a composite ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Credit revenue from operations comes from the Statement of Profit and Loss, and trade receivables come from the Balance Sheet. A ratio mixing the two statements is a composite ratio.\nStatement II is false. Gross profit and revenue from operations both come from the Statement of Profit and Loss. So the gross profit ratio is a Statement of Profit and Loss ratio, not a composite ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the interest coverage ratio:\nNet profit after tax: Rs. 84,000\nTax rate: 30%\n10% Debentures: Rs. 4,00,000","image":"","options":["3 times","4 times","3.1 times","2.1 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = Rs. 84,000; tax rate = 30%; 10% Debentures = Rs. 4,00,000.\nNPBT = PAT × 100/(100 − 30) = 84,000 × 100/70 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nInterest = 10% × 4,00,000 = Rs. 40,000.\nPBIT = 1,20,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nICR = 1,60,000 ÷ 40,000 = 4 times. The 3.1 times option comes from forgetting to gross up the tax.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has long-term debts of Rs. 4,00,000 and shareholders' funds of Rs. 8,00,000. It issues further equity shares of Rs. 2,00,000 for cash. What is the debt-equity ratio after the issue?","image":"","options":["0.4 : 1","0.5 : 1","0.33 : 1","0.6 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Debt = Rs. 4,00,000. Equity before issue = Rs. 8,00,000, so the original ratio is 0.5 : 1.\nAfter the issue, equity = 8,00,000 + 2,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000. Debt is unchanged.\nFormula: D/E = Long-term debts / Shareholders' funds = 4,00,000 / 10,00,000.\nSo the debt-equity ratio = 0.4 : 1, a decrease from 0.5 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meher Ltd. has revenue from operations of Rs. 5,00,000 and earns a gross profit of 25% on cost. Its inventory turnover ratio is 5 times, and closing inventory is 1.5 times the opening inventory. At year-end, total current assets (including closing inventory) are Rs. 2,46,000 and current liabilities are Rs. 1,00,000. There are no prepaid expenses or advance tax. Calculate the quick ratio at year-end.","image":"","options":["1.5 : 1","1.82 : 1","1.56 : 1","2.46 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"COGS = 5,00,000 × 100/125 = Rs. 4,00,000.\nAverage inventory = 4,00,000 ÷ 5 = Rs. 80,000.\nLet opening inventory = x. Then (x + 1.5x) ÷ 2 = 80,000, so x = Rs. 64,000 and closing inventory = Rs. 96,000.\nQuick assets = 2,46,000 − 96,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nQuick ratio = 1,50,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = 1.5 : 1.\nDeducting opening inventory instead gives the wrong answer of 1.82 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Under the traditional classification of ratios, a ratio computed with one variable from the Statement of Profit and Loss and the other from the Balance Sheet is called a composite ratio. Which of the following is a composite ratio?","image":"","options":["Current ratio","Gross profit ratio","Trade receivables turnover ratio","Operating ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The trade receivables turnover ratio is credit revenue from operations divided by average trade receivables. Credit revenue comes from the Statement of Profit and Loss, and trade receivables come from the Balance Sheet, so it is a composite ratio.\nThe current ratio uses only Balance Sheet figures, so it is a Balance Sheet ratio.\nThe gross profit ratio and the operating ratio use only Statement of Profit and Loss figures, so they are Statement of Profit and Loss ratios.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Sunrise Ltd. compares its current ratio for 2022-23, 2023-24 and 2024-25 to see how its liquidity has changed over time. This type of comparison is known as:","image":"","options":["Intra-firm comparison (Time Series Analysis)","Inter-firm comparison (Cross-sectional Analysis)","Comparison with industry standards","Common size analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When a firm compares its own ratios over several accounting periods, it is called intra-firm comparison, or time series analysis.\nInter-firm (cross-sectional) comparison means comparing with other enterprises, so it does not fit here.\nComparison with standards uses benchmarks set for the firm or industry.\nCommon size analysis is a different tool, not a type of ratio comparison.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are shown under current assets when computing the current ratio?\nCurrent investments, Inventories, Prepaid expenses, Advance tax, Long-term loans and advances, Non-current investments, Trade receivables, Short-term loans and advances","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Current assets include current investments, inventories, trade receivables, cash and cash equivalents, short-term loans and advances, and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax and accrued income.\nCurrent assets in the list: current investments, inventories, prepaid expenses, advance tax, trade receivables, and short-term loans and advances. That is 6 items.\nNot current: long-term loans and advances and non-current investments are non-current assets.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Purchase of goods on credit increases the debt-equity ratio of a company.\nReason (R): Trade payables arising from credit purchases are current liabilities and do not form part of long-term debt.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. The debt-equity ratio = Long-term debts / Shareholders' funds. A credit purchase affects neither of these, so the ratio stays unchanged. \nR is true. Creditors arising from credit purchases are trade payables, which are current liabilities. They are not long-term debt. \nThis is exactly why the ratio does not change. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The quick ratio of a firm is 1.5 : 1. Which of the following transactions will REDUCE its quick ratio?","image":"","options":["Cash collected from debtors","Payment made to a creditor by cheque","Purchase of goods on credit","Issue of equity shares for cash"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assume quick assets = Rs. 75,000 and current liabilities = Rs. 50,000.\n(1) Collection from debtors: one quick asset replaces another, so there is no change.\n(2) Payment of Rs. 10,000 to a creditor: 65,000/40,000 = 1.625, so the ratio rises.\n(3) Credit purchase of Rs. 10,000: inventory is not a quick asset, but creditors rise. The ratio becomes 75,000/60,000 = 1.25, so it falls.\n(4) Issue of shares for cash: quick assets rise, so the ratio improves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Arora Ltd. has a current ratio of 2.8 : 1, a quick ratio of 1.5 : 1 and working capital of Rs. 1,08,000. Inventory is its only non-quick current asset. Calculate the value of its inventory.","image":"","options":["Rs. 78,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 1,08,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current ratio = 2.8 : 1; Quick ratio = 1.5 : 1; Working capital = Rs. 1,08,000.\nLet current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 2.8x.\nWorking capital = 2.8x − x = 1.8x = 1,08,000, so x = Rs. 60,000.\nCurrent assets = 2.8 × 60,000 = Rs. 1,68,000.\nQuick assets = 1.5 × 60,000 = Rs. 90,000.\nInventory = 1,68,000 − 90,000 = Rs. 78,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The debt-equity ratio of a company is 0.5 : 1.\nAssertion (A): Purchase of goods on credit will increase the debt-equity ratio.\nReason (R): Trade payables are current liabilities and do not form part of long-term debts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true. Trade payables arise from credit purchases and are shown under current liabilities, not under long-term debt.\nA is false. A credit purchase raises inventory and trade payables only. Long-term debt and shareholders' funds are both unchanged, so the debt-equity ratio does not change.\nNCERT Illustration 10 reaches the same conclusion.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Profit after tax of Sita Ltd. is ₹3,00,000. Its share capital consists of 50,000 equity shares of ₹10 each and ₹5,00,000 of 10% preference shares. Calculate the earnings per share.","image":"","options":["₹6.00","₹5.00","₹4.17","₹0.50"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = ₹3,00,000; preference capital = ₹5,00,000 at 10%; 50,000 equity shares\nPreference dividend = 10% of 5,00,000 = ₹50,000\nProfit available for equity shareholders = 3,00,000 − 50,000 = ₹2,50,000\nFormula: EPS = Profit available for equity shareholders / Number of equity shares\nSubstituting: EPS = 2,50,000 / 50,000 = ₹5.00\n₹6 is wrong because it ignores the preference dividend.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a company is 2 : 1. How many of the following transactions will improve the current ratio?\n(i) Payment of unclaimed dividend\n(ii) Sale of goods costing Rs. 10,000 for Rs. 11,000 in cash\n(iii) Purchase of goods on credit\n(iv) Sale of a computer (book value Rs. 4,000) for Rs. 3,000\n(v) Cash received from trade receivables","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Improves. Unclaimed dividend is a current liability, so cash and CL fall equally, and an equal decrease raises a ratio above 1.\n(ii) Improves. CA rises by the profit of Rs. 1,000.\n(iii) Reduces. CA and CL rise equally, which lowers a ratio above 1.\n(iv) Improves. A fixed asset is turned into Rs. 3,000 cash, a current asset.\n(v) No change. One current asset is simply converted into another.\nSo three transactions, (i), (ii) and (iv), improve the ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The role of accounting ratios is essentially indicative, like that of a whistle blower.\nStatement II: Accounting ratios are an end in themselves, so calculating them completes the analysis of a business.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: NCERT describes the role of ratios as indicative, like a whistle blower that signals problem areas and bright spots.\nStatement II is false: NCERT stresses that ratios are a means to an end, not an end in themselves.\nAfter calculating ratios, management must still investigate the causes and take action.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company's net profit before interest and tax is Rs. 3,60,000. Interest on its long-term debts is Rs. 60,000. Calculate its interest coverage ratio.","image":"","options":["5 times","6 times","7 times","4 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: PBIT = Rs. 3,60,000; interest on long-term debts = Rs. 60,000.\nFormula: ICR = PBIT ÷ Interest on long-term debts.\nSubstituting: 3,60,000 ÷ 60,000 = 6.\nSo ICR = 6 times. The figure of 5 times would come from wrongly deducting the interest from PBIT first.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company's Debt-Equity Ratio is 0.5 : 1. Its total assets are Rs. 15,00,000 and its current liabilities are Rs. 3,00,000. Assuming capital employed consists only of long-term debts and shareholders' funds, calculate the amount of Long-term Debts.","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,00,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 4,00,000","Rs. 8,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets = 15,00,000; CL = 3,00,000; Debt = 0.5 × Equity.\nCapital employed (net assets) = 15,00,000 − 3,00,000 = Rs. 12,00,000.\nDebt + Equity = 12,00,000, so 0.5E + E = 12,00,000 and E = Rs. 8,00,000.\nSo Long-term Debts = 0.5 × 8,00,000 = Rs. 4,00,000.\nCheck: 4,00,000 / 8,00,000 = 0.5 : 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"On what basis are ratios grouped under the functional classification?","image":"","options":["The purpose for which the ratio is computed","The financial statement from which the figures are taken","The accounting period to which the figures relate","The industry to which the business belongs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the functional classification as being based on the purpose for which a ratio is computed. These purposes are liquidity, solvency, activity and profitability.\nGrouping by the financial statement the figures come from is the traditional classification, so option (2) is the tempting wrong choice.\nOptions (3) and (4) are not bases of classification in NCERT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of ratio analysis?\n(i) Ignores price-level changes\n(ii) Enables SWOT analysis\n(iii) Ignores qualitative or non-monetary aspects\n(iv) Lack of standardised definitions\n(v) Simplifies complex accounting figures\n(vi) Variations in accounting practices among firms","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Limitation: accounts follow the stable money assumption and ignore inflation.\n(ii) Advantage, not a limitation.\n(iii) Limitation: ratios reflect only monetary aspects.\n(iv) Limitation: for example, 'liquid liabilities' has no standard definition.\n(v) Advantage, not a limitation.\n(vi) Limitation: differing accounting policies weaken inter-firm comparison.\nSo there are 4 limitations: (i), (iii), (iv) and (vi).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Kavya Enterprises has liquid assets of Rs. 1,20,000 and a quick ratio of 2 : 1. Its other current assets are inventory of Rs. 45,000 and prepaid expenses of Rs. 3,000. The management wants to raise the quick ratio to 3 : 1 by paying off some trade payables in cash.\nWhat amount must be paid to the creditors?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Liquid assets = Rs. 1,20,000; Quick ratio = 2 : 1. So current liabilities = 1,20,000 / 2 = Rs. 60,000.\nInventory and prepaid expenses are not quick assets, so they do not affect the calculation.\nLet the payment be p. Paying creditors in cash reduces both liquid assets and current liabilities by p.\nSet up: (1,20,000 − p)/(60,000 − p) = 3, which gives 1,20,000 − p = 1,80,000 − 3p.\nSo 2p = 60,000 and p = Rs. 30,000.\nCheck: 90,000/30,000 = 3 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The net profit after tax of Vikas Ltd. for the year is ₹1,80,000, and the tax rate is 40%. Its long-term funds consist of equity share capital of ₹8,00,000, reserves and surplus of ₹2,00,000 and 12% debentures of ₹5,00,000. Calculate the Return on Investment (Return on Capital Employed).","image":"","options":["12%","20%","36%","24%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: PAT = ₹1,80,000; tax rate = 40%\nPBT = PAT × 100 / (100 − 40) = 1,80,000 × 100 / 60 = ₹3,00,000\nDebenture interest = 12% of 5,00,000 = ₹60,000, so PBIT = 3,00,000 + 60,000 = ₹3,60,000\nCapital employed = 8,00,000 + 2,00,000 + 5,00,000 = ₹15,00,000\nSubstituting: ROI = 3,60,000 / 15,00,000 × 100 = 24%\n12% and 20% are wrong because they use PAT or PBT instead of PBIT.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Formula):\nList-I\nA. Interest coverage ratio\nB. Inventory turnover ratio\nC. Return on investment\nD. Proprietary ratio\nList-II\n1. Shareholders' funds / Capital employed\n2. Net profit before interest and tax / Interest on long-term debts\n3. Cost of revenue from operations / Average inventory\n4. Profit before interest and tax / Capital employed × 100","image":"","options":["A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-2: The interest coverage ratio shows how many times the profit before interest and tax covers the interest on long-term debt.\nB-3: The inventory turnover ratio uses cost of revenue from operations, not revenue, over average inventory.\nC-4: Return on investment is PBIT as a percentage of capital employed.\nD-1: The proprietary ratio is shareholders' funds divided by capital employed (net assets).\nOption 2 is tempting because 2 and 4 both use PBIT. However, interest coverage divides by interest, while ROI divides by capital employed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has current assets of Rs. 60,000 and current liabilities of Rs. 30,000. It sells an old machine with a book value of Rs. 8,000 for Rs. 6,000 in cash. What is the current ratio after this transaction?","image":"","options":["2.2 : 1","2.27 : 1","1.8 : 1","2 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CA = Rs. 60,000, CL = Rs. 30,000. The machine is a fixed asset, so only the cash received of Rs. 6,000 enters current assets.\nNew CA = 60,000 + 6,000 = Rs. 66,000. CL is unchanged.\nFormula: Current ratio = CA / CL = 66,000 / 30,000.\nSo the current ratio = 2.2 : 1, which is an improvement even though the sale was at a loss.\nUsing the book value of Rs. 8,000 (giving 2.27) is wrong, because only the cash actually received is added.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Kavya Ltd. is Rs. 4,80,000 and the gross profit ratio is 25% on revenue from operations. Its inventory turnover ratio is 6 times. Closing inventory exceeds opening inventory by Rs. 16,000. Calculate the closing inventory.","image":"","options":["Rs. 52,000","Rs. 76,000","Rs. 68,000","Rs. 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"COGS = 4,80,000 − 25% of 4,80,000 = Rs. 3,60,000.\nAverage inventory = 3,60,000 ÷ 6 = Rs. 60,000.\nLet opening inventory = x. Then (x + x + 16,000) ÷ 2 = 60,000, so x + 8,000 = 60,000 and x = Rs. 52,000.\nClosing inventory = 52,000 + 16,000 = Rs. 68,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The earnings per share of Y Ltd. are Rs. 4 and the market price of its equity share is Rs. 60. What is its Price/Earning ratio?","image":"","options":["15 times","0.067 times","64 times","240 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Market price per share = Rs. 60; EPS = Rs. 4.\nFormula: P/E Ratio = Market Price of a Share / Earnings per Share.\nSubstituting: 60 / 4 = 15 times.\nThis means investors are paying 15 times the current earnings, which reflects their expectation of future growth. 0.067 comes from inverting the formula.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Prakash Ltd., calculate the inventory turnover ratio:\nOpening inventory Rs. 30,000; Purchases Rs. 1,70,000; Wages Rs. 12,000; Carriage inwards Rs. 8,000; Carriage outwards Rs. 5,000; Closing inventory Rs. 40,000; Revenue from operations Rs. 2,80,000.","image":"","options":["5.29 times","5.14 times","8 times","4.91 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"COGS = Opening inventory + Purchases + Wages + Carriage inwards − Closing inventory.\nSubstituting: 30,000 + 1,70,000 + 12,000 + 8,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 1,80,000. Carriage outwards is a selling expense, so it is excluded.\nAverage inventory = (30,000 + 40,000) ÷ 2 = Rs. 35,000.\nITR = 1,80,000 ÷ 35,000 = 5.14 times.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Arun Ltd. has 1,00,000 equity shares of ₹10 each and no preference shares. Its profit after tax is ₹2,50,000. It declared a dividend of 15%, and the market price of a share is ₹30. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) EPS is ₹2.50\n(ii) Dividend payout ratio is 0.6\n(iii) Price/earning ratio is 12\n(iv) A dividend payout ratio of 0.6 means the company distributes 40% of its earnings and retains 60%","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: EPS = 2,50,000 / 1,00,000 = ₹2.50.\n(ii) Correct: DPS = 15% of ₹10 = ₹1.50, so the payout ratio = 1.50 / 2.50 = 0.6.\n(iii) Correct: P/E ratio = 30 / 2.50 = 12.\n(iv) Incorrect: a payout ratio of 0.6 means 60% of earnings are distributed and 40% are retained.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Kaveri Ltd. has an interest coverage ratio of 6 times. Its net profit after tax is Rs. 1,50,000 and the tax rate is 25%. The only long-term debt is 10% Debentures. Calculate the amount of 10% Debentures.","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 3,33,333","Rs. 3,75,000","Rs. 4,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: ICR = 6; PAT = Rs. 1,50,000; tax rate = 25%; debentures carry 10% interest.\nNPBT = 1,50,000 × 100/75 = Rs. 2,00,000.\nLet the interest be I. Then PBIT = 2,00,000 + I, and ICR gives PBIT = 6I.\nSo 2,00,000 + I = 6I, which gives 5I = 2,00,000 and I = Rs. 40,000.\nDebentures = 40,000 ÷ 10% = Rs. 4,00,000.\nTaking 6I = 2,00,000 directly gives Rs. 3,33,333, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about activity ratios are correct?\n(i) A higher trade receivables turnover ratio indicates speedier collection from customers.\n(ii) While calculating trade receivables turnover, debtors are taken after deducting the provision for doubtful debts.\n(iii) When the amount of credit purchases is not given, net purchases are used to calculate the trade payables turnover ratio.\n(iv) Capital employed turnover is analysed further through fixed assets turnover and working capital turnover.\n(v) Cash revenue from operations is included in the numerator of the trade receivables turnover ratio.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: higher turnover means receivables are realised faster.\n(ii) Incorrect: debtors are taken before making any provision for doubtful debts.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT treats purchases as net purchases when credit purchase data is absent.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT analyses capital employed turnover through these two ratios.\n(v) Incorrect: only net credit revenue from operations is used.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of ratio analysis that arise from the nature of financial statements (and not limitations of the ratios themselves)?\n• Ignores price-level changes\n• Lack of standardised definitions\n• Variations in accounting practices\n• Means and not the end\n• Forecasting\n• Lack of universally accepted standard levels","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Limitations arising from the nature of financial statements include price-level changes, variations in accounting practices and forecasting. That makes 3.\nLimitations of ratios themselves include means and not the end, lack of standardised definitions and lack of universally accepted standard levels.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Basis of computation):\nList-I\n(A) Trade Receivables Turnover\n(B) Trade Payables Turnover\n(C) Working Capital Turnover\n(D) Net Assets Turnover\nList-II\n(i) Revenue from operations ÷ (Current assets − Current liabilities)\n(ii) Net credit purchases ÷ Average trade payables\n(iii) Revenue from operations ÷ (Shareholders' funds + Long-term debts)\n(iv) Net credit revenue from operations ÷ Average trade receivables","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Trade receivables arise from credit sales, so the ratio is credit revenue ÷ average trade receivables → (iv).\n(B) Trade payables arise from credit purchases → (ii).\n(C) Working capital equals current assets minus current liabilities → (i).\n(D) Net assets, or capital employed, equal shareholders' funds plus long-term debts → (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Kabir Ltd. for the year is Rs. 3,60,000. Goods are sold at a gross profit of 20% on cost. Inventory in the beginning was Rs. 35,000 and inventory at the end was Rs. 45,000. Calculate the Inventory Turnover Ratio.","image":"","options":["7.5 times","7.2 times","9 times","6.67 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue Rs. 3,60,000 and gross profit of 20% on cost. This means that if cost is 100, the selling price is 120.\nCost of revenue from operations = 3,60,000 × 100/120 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nAverage inventory = (35,000 + 45,000)/2 = Rs. 40,000.\nInventory Turnover Ratio = 3,00,000/40,000 = 7.5 times.\nThe tempting option 7.2 times is wrong. It treats the 20% as a margin on sales (cost = Rs. 2,88,000).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Quick ratio is considered a better measure of the liquidity position of a business than the current ratio.\nReason (R): While computing quick assets, inventories and prepaid expenses are excluded from current assets because they are not quickly convertible into cash.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the quick ratio is a better measure of liquidity than the current ratio and serves as an acid test.\nR is true. Inventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax are non-liquid current assets, so they are left out of quick assets.\nR explains A. Because these non-liquid items are excluded, the quick ratio reflects the firm's ability to pay current liabilities more strictly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"While calculating the quick (liquid) ratio, which one of the following current assets is excluded from quick assets?","image":"","options":["Trade receivables","Prepaid expenses","Current investments","Cash and cash equivalents"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Quick assets are current assets that can be converted into cash quickly.\nAccording to NCERT, inventories and other current assets such as prepaid expenses and advance tax are excluded.\nPrepaid expenses cannot be turned into cash, because the money has already been spent on a future service, so they are left out.\nTrade receivables, current investments and cash equivalents are all quickly realisable, so they stay in quick assets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"It is said that accounting ratios are 'means to an end rather than the end in themselves'. Which statement best explains this?","image":"","options":["Ratios give the final, precise solution to a firm's problems","Ratios indicate problem areas like a whistle-blower, but management must still investigate and act","Ratios are useful only at the end of the accounting period","Ratios replace the need to prepare financial statements"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the role of ratios as essentially indicative, like a whistle-blower.\nRatios point towards areas that need attention. They do not by themselves resolve the problem, so further analysis and decisions are needed.\nOption (1) is tempting but wrong. Ratios cannot resolve problems, and they are not precise or final.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are EXCLUDED while computing the Operating Ratio of a company?\n(i) Loss on sale of a fixed asset\n(ii) Interest paid on debentures\n(iii) Depreciation\n(iv) Dividend received\n(v) Loss of stock by fire\n(vi) Selling and distribution expenses\n(vii) Speculation gain","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The operating ratio considers only the cost of operations. Non-operating incomes and expenses are excluded.\n(i) Excluded: non-operating loss.\n(ii) Excluded: a financial charge.\n(iii) Included: an operating expense.\n(iv) Excluded: non-operating income.\n(v) Excluded: an abnormal, non-operating loss.\n(vi) Included: an operating expense.\n(vii) Excluded: non-operating income.\nSo five items are excluded.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Total purchases of Kiran Stores during the year were Rs. 8,00,000, of which 75% were on credit. Creditors were Rs. 1,10,000 at the beginning and Rs. 90,000 at the end of the year. Bills payable were Rs. 30,000 at the beginning and Rs. 70,000 at the end. Calculate the average payment period in months.","image":"","options":["3 months","2.25 months","2 months","4 months"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Credit purchases = 75% of 8,00,000 = Rs. 6,00,000.\nAverage trade payables = [(1,10,000 + 30,000) + (90,000 + 70,000)]/2 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nTrade Payables Turnover = 6,00,000 / 1,50,000 = 4 times.\nAverage payment period = 12 / 4 = 3 months.\nUsing total purchases gives 5.33 times and 2.25 months (wrong). Ignoring bills payable gives 2 months (wrong).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio of a firm is 5 times. Taking a year of 365 days, what is the average collection period?","image":"","options":["73 days","72 days","60 days","1,825 days"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: TR Turnover = 5 times and the year has 365 days.\nFormula: Average collection period = Number of days in a year / TR Turnover Ratio.\nSubstituting: 365 / 5 = 73.\nSo the average collection period = 73 days. The option 72 days would come from a 360-day year, which is not the basis given here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If ratio analysis shows a fall in net profit ratio, the ratio itself provides the solution needed to restore profitability.\nStatement II: There is a universal yardstick that specifies the ideal level of each ratio for all firms and industries.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Ratios only indicate and blow the whistle; NCERT lists 'lack of ability to resolve problems' as a limitation.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states there is no universal yardstick and no universally acceptable list of ideal ratio levels.\nNorms such as 2 : 1 for the current ratio are only general guides and vary across industries.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"As per NCERT, the interest coverage ratio is computed by dividing which profit figure by the interest on long-term debts?","image":"","options":["Net profit after tax","Net profit before tax","Net profit before interest and tax","Gross profit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Interest Coverage Ratio = Net Profit before Interest and Tax ÷ Interest on long-term debts.\nInterest is paid out of profit before it is reduced by interest and tax. So the profit actually available to pay interest is the PBIT figure.\nNet profit before tax is the most tempting wrong option. It has already been reduced by interest, so using it would understate the cover.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Meera Traders for 2024-25, calculate the Inventory Turnover Ratio:\nInventory in the beginning: Rs. 30,000\nInventory at the end: Rs. 50,000\nNet purchases: Rs. 1,50,000\nWages: Rs. 20,000\nCarriage inwards: Rs. 10,000\nRevenue from operations: Rs. 2,40,000","image":"","options":["4 times","3.2 times","3.75 times","5.33 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Opening inventory Rs. 30,000, purchases Rs. 1,50,000, wages Rs. 20,000, carriage inwards Rs. 10,000 and closing inventory Rs. 50,000.\nCost of revenue from operations = 30,000 + 1,50,000 + 20,000 + 10,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nAverage inventory = (30,000 + 50,000)/2 = Rs. 40,000.\nInventory Turnover Ratio = 1,60,000/40,000 = 4 times.\nUsing only the closing inventory (Rs. 50,000) gives 3.2 times, which is wrong. Leaving out carriage inwards gives 3.75 times, which is also wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Meera Traders provides the following information:\nTotal current assets Rs. 1,20,000 (including Inventory Rs. 30,000, Prepaid expenses Rs. 6,000 and Advance tax Rs. 4,000)\nCurrent liabilities Rs. 50,000\nCalculate the quick ratio.","image":"","options":["1.6 : 1","1.8 : 1","2.4 : 1","1.72 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current assets = Rs. 1,20,000; Inventory = Rs. 30,000; Prepaid expenses = Rs. 6,000; Advance tax = Rs. 4,000; Current liabilities = Rs. 50,000.\nFormula: Quick assets = Current assets − (Inventory + Prepaid expenses + Advance tax).\nSubstituting: Quick assets = 1,20,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nQuick ratio = 80,000 / 50,000 = 1.6 : 1.\nThe option 1.8 : 1 is a trap: it removes only inventory and wrongly keeps prepaid expenses and advance tax.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. has current assets of Rs. 1,20,000 and current liabilities of Rs. 60,000. The management wants to raise the current ratio to 3 : 1 by paying off some trade payables in cash. How much should it pay to the creditors?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CA = Rs. 1,20,000, CL = Rs. 60,000, present ratio = 2 : 1, target = 3 : 1.\nLet the payment be x. Both CA and CL fall by x.\nFormula: (1,20,000 − x)/(60,000 − x) = 3.\nSolving: 1,20,000 − x = 1,80,000 − 3x, so 2x = 60,000 and x = Rs. 30,000.\nCheck: 90,000/30,000 = 3 : 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a company is 2 : 1. Match the transactions in List-I with their effect on the current ratio in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Payment to trade payables\n(B) Purchase of goods on credit\n(C) Cash collected from trade receivables\n(D) Sale of goods costing Rs. 10,000 for Rs. 12,000 in cash\nList-II\n(i) Improves\n(ii) Reduces\n(iii) No change","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Equal decrease in CA and CL raises a ratio above 1, so it improves: (i).\n(B) Equal increase in CA and CL lowers a ratio above 1, so it reduces: (ii).\n(C) Debtors fall and cash rises, so total CA is unchanged: (iii).\n(D) Inventory falls by Rs. 10,000 and cash rises by Rs. 12,000. CA increases by Rs. 2,000 with CL unchanged, so it improves: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A debt-equity ratio of 2 : 1 is normally considered safe, although the acceptable level may vary from industry to industry.\nStatement II: If net assets (capital employed) are used instead of total assets, the Total Assets to Debt Ratio becomes the reciprocal of the Debt to Capital Employed Ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT calls 2 : 1 normally safe but notes that it may vary from industry to industry.\nStatement II is correct: with net assets, the ratio is Capital Employed / Long-term Debt. This is the exact reciprocal of Long-term Debt / Capital Employed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Nilgiri Ltd. reports the following for 2024-25:\nNet profit after tax: Rs. 1,50,000\nTax paid: Rs. 50,000\n10% Debentures: Rs. 4,00,000\n12% Preference share capital: Rs. 1,00,000\nThe accountant included preference dividend along with debenture interest in the denominator and reported the interest coverage ratio as 4.62 times. What is the correct interest coverage ratio?","image":"","options":["4.62 times","4.75 times","6 times","5 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Debenture interest = 10% × 4,00,000 = Rs. 40,000.\nPBIT = PAT + Tax + Interest = 1,50,000 + 50,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nPreference dividend is an appropriation of profit, not interest on long-term debt. So it is excluded from the denominator.\nICR = 2,40,000 ÷ 40,000 = 6 times.\nThe accountant's 4.62 comes from 2,40,000 ÷ 52,000. The 4.75 option ignores the tax added back.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Category under functional classification):\nList-I\n(a) Quick ratio\n(b) Interest coverage ratio\n(c) Working capital turnover ratio\n(d) Operating ratio\nList-II\n(i) Profitability ratio\n(ii) Activity ratio\n(iii) Liquidity ratio\n(iv) Solvency ratio","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Quick ratio measures short-term ability to pay, so it is a liquidity ratio (iii).\n(b) Interest coverage ratio measures the safety of interest on long-term debt, so it is a solvency ratio (iv).\n(c) Working capital turnover measures efficiency in using resources, so it is an activity ratio (ii).\n(d) Operating ratio relates operating cost to revenue, so it is a profitability ratio (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the Inventory Turnover Ratio:\nRevenue from operations: Rs. 6,00,000\nGross profit: 25% on cost of revenue from operations\nInventory in the beginning: Rs. 40,000\nInventory at the end is Rs. 20,000 more than inventory in the beginning","image":"","options":["9 times","9.6 times","12 times","8 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = Rs. 6,00,000, gross profit = 25% on cost. \nCost of revenue from operations = 6,00,000 × 100/125 = Rs. 4,80,000. \nClosing inventory = 40,000 + 20,000 = Rs. 60,000. \nAverage inventory = (40,000 + 60,000)/2 = Rs. 50,000. \nInventory Turnover Ratio = 4,80,000 / 50,000 = 9.6 times. \nOption (1), 9 times, is wrong: it takes the 25% profit on revenue instead of on cost. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Advantage of ratio analysis) with List-II (Description):\nList-I\nA. Helps to understand efficacy of decisions\nB. Simplifies complex figures\nC. Helpful in comparative analysis\nD. Enables SWOT analysis\nList-II\n(i) Explains changes so that management understands current threats and opportunities\n(ii) Shows whether operating, investing and financing decisions improved performance\n(iii) Many years' figures kept side by side reveal trends useful for projections\n(iv) Summarises financial information and helps assess credit worthiness and earning capacity","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Efficacy of decisions means judging whether the operating, investing and financing decisions were the right ones.\nB-(iv): Simplifying figures means summarising financial information and assessing credit worthiness and earning capacity.\nC-(iii): Comparative analysis keeps several years' figures side by side to find trends and make projections.\nD-(i): SWOT analysis uses information about changes to identify current threats and opportunities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Under the traditional classification of ratios, how is the Inventory Turnover Ratio (Cost of Revenue from Operations ÷ Average Inventory) classified?","image":"","options":["Balance sheet ratio","Statement of profit and loss ratio","Composite ratio","Profitability ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The traditional classification groups ratios by the statement their two figures come from.\nCost of revenue from operations comes from the statement of profit and loss.\nInventory comes from the balance sheet.\nA ratio that uses one figure from each statement is a composite ratio.\n'Profitability ratio' is a functional category, not a traditional one. Inventory turnover is in any case an activity ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Its only current asset that is not a quick asset is inventory, valued at Rs. 72,000. What are its current assets?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,80,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 1,08,000","Rs. 1,20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: current ratio = 3 : 1, quick ratio = 1.8 : 1, inventory = Rs. 72,000.\nLet current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 3x and quick assets = 1.8x.\nInventory = current assets − quick assets = 3x − 1.8x = 1.2x.\nSo 1.2x = 72,000, which gives x = Rs. 60,000.\nCurrent assets = 3 × 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 2.5 : 1 and current liabilities of Rs. 2,00,000. Its revenue from operations for the year is Rs. 18,00,000. Calculate the Working Capital Turnover Ratio.","image":"","options":["6 times","3.6 times","9 times","2.57 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current ratio = 2.5 : 1 and CL = Rs. 2,00,000.\nCurrent assets = 2.5 × 2,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000.\nWorking capital = 5,00,000 − 2,00,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nFormula: WC Turnover = Revenue from operations / Working capital = 18,00,000 / 3,00,000.\nSo the ratio = 6 times. Dividing by current assets (3.6) or by current liabilities (9) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are current liabilities?\n(i) Bills payable\n(ii) Bank overdraft\n(iii) Short-term provisions\n(iv) Long-term provisions\n(v) Advance tax\n(vi) Unclaimed dividend\n(vii) Creditors","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Bills payable: current liability (a trade payable).\n(ii) Bank overdraft: current liability (short-term borrowing).\n(iii) Short-term provisions: current liability.\n(iv) Long-term provisions: non-current liability.\n(v) Advance tax: current asset.\n(vi) Unclaimed dividend: current liability, as treated in NCERT Illustration 6.\n(vii) Creditors: current liability.\nTotal current liabilities = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to the inventory turnover ratio:\nList-I\n(A) Numerator of inventory turnover ratio\n(B) Average inventory\n(C) Cost of revenue from operations\n(D) A very high inventory turnover ratio\nList-II\n(i) Revenue from operations − Gross profit\n(ii) May result from buying in small lots\n(iii) Cost of revenue from operations\n(iv) (Opening inventory + Closing inventory) ÷ 2\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(A)-(i), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(ii)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(i), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): Inventory turnover ratio = Cost of revenue from operations ÷ Average inventory, so the numerator is cost of revenue from operations.\n(B)-(iv): Average inventory is the arithmetic average of opening and closing inventory.\n(C)-(i): Cost of revenue from operations = Revenue from operations − Gross profit.\n(D)-(ii): A high turnover is good, but it must be read with care. It may come from buying in small lots or from selling quickly at a low margin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the operating ratio:\nStatement I: Loss by fire is excluded from operating cost while computing the operating ratio.\nStatement II: A higher operating ratio indicates better operational efficiency of the business.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: True. Operating cost excludes non-operating items such as loss by fire, loss on sale of assets, interest paid and dividend received.\nStatement II: False. The operating ratio shows cost of operations as a percentage of revenue from operations.\nA higher operating ratio means a smaller operating margin. NCERT calls a lower operating ratio a healthy sign.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The Debt-Equity Ratio of a company is 0.6 : 1. Which of the following transactions will INCREASE this ratio?","image":"","options":["Conversion of debentures into equity shares","Purchase of machinery by issuing debentures to the vendor","Redemption of debentures in cash","Purchase of goods on credit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Debt falls and equity rises, so the ratio decreases.\n(2) Debentures are long-term debt, so debt rises while equity is unchanged. The ratio increases.\n(3) Debt falls, so the ratio decreases.\n(4) Only current assets and current liabilities change. Neither debt nor equity is affected, so there is no change.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Nexa Ltd. reported revenue from operations of Rs. 5,00,000 and gross profit of Rs. 60,000, giving a gross profit ratio of 12%. It was later found that closing inventory had been overstated by Rs. 15,000. This shows that a ratio is only as reliable as the figures it is based on. What is the correct gross profit ratio?","image":"","options":["9%","12%","15%","10.5%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 5,00,000; Reported gross profit = Rs. 60,000; Closing inventory overstated by Rs. 15,000\nLogic: Overstating closing inventory understates cost of revenue, so gross profit is overstated by Rs. 15,000.\nCorrect gross profit = 60,000 − 15,000 = Rs. 45,000\nFormula: Gross profit ratio = 45,000 / 5,00,000 × 100 = 9%\nThe 15% option wrongly adds the error instead of removing it. Errors in the basic figures produce misleading ratios.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The finance team of Meghna Ltd. extracted the following from its balance sheet:\nEquity share capital: Rs. 5,00,000\nGeneral reserve: Rs. 1,50,000\nShare application money pending allotment: Rs. 50,000\n12% Debentures: Rs. 3,50,000\nTrade payables: Rs. 1,20,000\nOutstanding expenses: Rs. 30,000\nThe company holds no other liabilities or equity items. Calculate the Total Assets to Debt Ratio.","image":"","options":["2.4 : 1","3 : 1","3.43 : 1","3.29 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Total assets = total of equity and liabilities = 5,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 50,000 + 3,50,000 + 1,20,000 + 30,000 = Rs. 12,00,000.\nLong-term debt = 12% Debentures = Rs. 3,50,000. Trade payables and outstanding expenses are current liabilities, not long-term debt.\nRatio = 12,00,000 / 3,50,000 = 3.43 : 1.\nWhy the others fail:\n2.4 : 1 divides by total debts of Rs. 5,00,000.\n3 : 1 uses net assets.\n3.29 : 1 omits the share application money.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following, calculate the Inventory Turnover Ratio:\nRevenue from operations ₹ 6,00,000\nGross profit is 25% on cost of revenue from operations\nOpening inventory ₹ 70,000\nClosing inventory ₹ 90,000","image":"","options":["6 times","5.63 times","7.5 times","4.5 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = ₹ 6,00,000; gross profit = 25% on cost; opening inventory = ₹ 70,000; closing inventory = ₹ 90,000.\nCost of revenue from operations: profit is 25% on cost, so revenue = 125% of cost. Cost = 6,00,000 × 100/125 = ₹ 4,80,000.\nAverage inventory = (70,000 + 90,000)/2 = ₹ 80,000.\nInventory Turnover Ratio = Cost of revenue from operations / Average inventory = 4,80,000/80,000 = 6 times.\nOption (2) wrongly takes 25% of revenue as gross profit, giving 4,50,000/80,000 = 5.63 times.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Its working capital is Rs. 1,20,000. Inventory is the only current asset that is not a quick asset. Calculate the value of inventory.","image":"","options":["Rs. 72,000","Rs. 1,08,000","Rs. 48,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CA : CL = 3 : 1; Working capital = Rs. 1,20,000.\nWorking capital = 3x − x = 2x = 1,20,000, so x = current liabilities = Rs. 60,000.\nCurrent assets = 3 × 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nQuick assets = 1.8 × 60,000 = Rs. 1,08,000.\nInventory = Current assets − Quick assets = 1,80,000 − 1,08,000 = Rs. 72,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera Ltd. has a capital employed of Rs. 21,00,000 and a debt-equity ratio of 0.4 : 1. During the year it raises a further long-term loan of Rs. 3,00,000. Shareholders' funds remain unchanged. What will be the Debt to Capital Employed Ratio after the new loan?","image":"","options":["0.29 : 1","0.43 : 1","0.6 : 1","0.375 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: D/E = 0.4 : 1. Let equity be E, so debt = 0.4E.\nCapital employed = Debt + Equity = 1.4E = 21,00,000. So E = Rs. 15,00,000 and debt = Rs. 6,00,000.\nAfter the loan: Debt = 9,00,000 and Capital employed = 24,00,000.\nFormula: Debt to Capital Employed = Long-term debt / Capital employed = 9,00,000 / 24,00,000 = 0.375 : 1.\nThe option 0.43 : 1 is wrong because it forgets that capital employed also rises with the new loan.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Meera Ltd., calculate the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio:\nTotal revenue from operations: Rs. 5,00,000 (cash revenue is 20% of total revenue)\nDebtors: opening Rs. 45,000, closing Rs. 55,000\nBills receivable: opening Rs. 15,000, closing Rs. 25,000\nProvision for doubtful debts on closing debtors: Rs. 5,000","image":"","options":["5.71 times","5.93 times","7.14 times","8 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Cash revenue = 20% of 5,00,000 = Rs. 1,00,000, so credit revenue = Rs. 4,00,000.\nOpening TR = 45,000 + 15,000 = Rs. 60,000. Closing TR = 55,000 + 25,000 = Rs. 80,000. The provision is ignored.\nAverage TR = (60,000 + 80,000)/2 = Rs. 70,000.\nSubstituting: 4,00,000 / 70,000 = 5.71 times.\nDeducting the provision gives 5.93 (wrong). Using total revenue gives 7.14 (wrong).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The quick ratio is regarded as a better test of liquidity than the current ratio.\nReason (R): Inventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax are excluded when computing quick assets.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls the quick (acid-test) ratio a better measure of the liquidity position than the current ratio.\nR is true: quick assets are current assets minus inventories and other non-liquid items such as prepaid expenses and advance tax.\nR explains A: these items are removed because they cannot be turned into cash quickly. What remains is a stricter measure of the firm's ability to pay current liabilities.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A single ratio can be placed under both the traditional and the functional classification. Which of the following pairings is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Interest coverage ratio – Composite ratio; Solvency ratio","Debt-equity ratio – Balance Sheet ratio; Solvency ratio","Inventory turnover ratio – Composite ratio; Activity ratio","Gross profit ratio – Statement of Profit and Loss ratio; Profitability ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. Interest coverage = net profit before interest and tax ÷ interest on long-term debts. Both figures come from the Statement of Profit and Loss, so traditionally it is a P&L ratio, not a composite ratio. Functionally it is a solvency ratio.\n(2) Correct. Long-term debts and shareholders' funds are both Balance Sheet items, and the ratio measures solvency.\n(3) Correct. Cost of revenue (P&L) and average inventory (Balance Sheet) make it composite, and functionally it is an activity ratio.\n(4) Correct. Both figures come from the P&L, and it is a profitability ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has Revenue from Operations of Rs. 4,00,000, Cost of Revenue from Operations of Rs. 2,60,000 and Operating Expenses of Rs. 60,000. What is its Operating Ratio?","image":"","options":["65%","80%","20%","15%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 4,00,000; Cost of Revenue = Rs. 2,60,000; Operating Expenses = Rs. 60,000.\nFormula: Operating Ratio = (Cost of Revenue from Operations + Operating Expenses) / Revenue from Operations × 100.\nSubstituting: (2,60,000 + 60,000) / 4,00,000 × 100 = 3,20,000 / 4,00,000 × 100 = 80%.\n65% leaves out the operating expenses. 20% is the operating profit ratio (100 − 80).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Quick ratio is considered a better measure of the liquidity position of a business than current ratio.\nReason (R): While calculating quick assets, inventories and other current assets such as prepaid expenses and advance tax are excluded from current assets.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls the quick (acid-test) ratio a better measure of liquidity than the current ratio. \nR is true: inventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax are not quickly convertible into cash, so they are left out of quick assets. \nR explains A: because these non-liquid items are excluded, the quick ratio gives a sharper picture of the firm's ability to pay current liabilities. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A low inventory turnover ratio is regarded as a danger signal for a business.\nReason (R): A low inventory turnover may be the result of bad buying or the presence of obsolete inventory.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A low turnover means inventory moves slowly and funds stay blocked, so NCERT calls it a danger signal.\nR is true. NCERT says low turnover may be due to bad buying or obsolete inventory.\nR gives the reason why low turnover signals danger, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Category under functional classification):\nList-I\nA. Interest Coverage Ratio\nB. Quick Ratio\nC. Inventory Turnover Ratio\nD. Operating Ratio\nList-II\nI. Activity ratio\nII. Profitability ratio\nIII. Solvency ratio\nIV. Liquidity ratio","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Interest coverage measures the safety of interest on long-term debt, so it is a solvency ratio. \nB-IV: The quick ratio measures short-term debt-paying ability, so it is a liquidity ratio. \nC-I: Inventory turnover measures how efficiently inventory is used, so it is an activity ratio. \nD-II: The operating ratio relates operating cost to revenue from operations, so it is a profitability ratio. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Under the traditional classification of ratios, what is a ratio called when one of its variables is taken from the Statement of Profit and Loss and the other from the Balance Sheet?","image":"","options":["Statement of Profit and Loss ratio","Balance Sheet ratio","Composite ratio","Activity ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The traditional classification groups ratios by the financial statement their figures come from.\nIf both figures come from the Statement of Profit and Loss, it is a P&L ratio. If both come from the Balance Sheet, it is a Balance Sheet ratio.\nIf one figure comes from each statement, it is a composite ratio. An example is trade receivables turnover: credit revenue from operations (P&L) ÷ trade receivables (Balance Sheet).\n'Activity ratio' is a functional category, not a traditional one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the Average Payment Period (take 365 days in a year):\nTotal Purchases: Rs. 9,00,000\nCash Purchases: Rs. 1,70,000\nCreditors on 1 April: Rs. 1,20,000; Bills Payable on 1 April: Rs. 30,000\nCreditors on 31 March: Rs. 1,40,000; Bills Payable on 31 March: Rs. 70,000","image":"","options":["90 days","73 days","180 days","65 days"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Credit purchases = 9,00,000 − 1,70,000 = Rs. 7,30,000.\nOpening trade payables = 1,20,000 + 30,000 = Rs. 1,50,000. Closing trade payables = 1,40,000 + 70,000 = Rs. 2,10,000.\nAverage trade payables = (1,50,000 + 2,10,000)/2 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nTrade Payables Turnover = 7,30,000/1,80,000 = 4.056 times.\nAverage Payment Period = 365/4.056 = 90 days.\nOption (2) uses total purchases. Option (3) skips dividing by 2. Option (4) ignores bills payable.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A company has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Its only non-quick current asset is inventory of Rs. 48,000. Calculate its current assets.","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 72,000","Rs. 1,60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current ratio (CR) = 3 : 1; Quick ratio (QR) = 1.8 : 1; Inventory = Rs. 48,000.\nLet current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 3x and quick assets = 1.8x.\nInventory = Current assets − Quick assets, so 48,000 = 3x − 1.8x = 1.2x.\nSolving, x = Rs. 40,000, which is the current liabilities.\nCurrent assets = 3 × 40,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nThe option Rs. 40,000 is the current liabilities, not the current assets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has creditors of ₹1,00,000 and furniture of ₹1,00,000. A trainee calculates the ratio as 1 : 1 and uses it to judge the firm's solvency. The trainee's approach suffers from which limitation?","image":"","options":["Variations in accounting practices","Forecasting based on historical data","Ratio based on unrelated figures","Ignores qualitative aspects"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A ratio is meaningful only when the two figures are meaningfully correlated.\nCreditors (a current liability) and furniture (a fixed asset) have no logical relationship with each other.\nThe 1 : 1 ratio therefore says nothing about efficiency or solvency. This is the limitation of 'ratios based on unrelated figures'.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the case and answer.\nMeera, a financial analyst, studies Alpha Ltd. She:\n(P) compares Alpha's net profit ratio for 2024-25 with that of Beta Ltd., a firm in the same industry;\n(Q) compares Alpha's quick ratio with the norm of 1:1 expected in the industry;\n(R) compares Alpha's inventory turnover ratio over the last five years.\nWhich option correctly identifies the types of comparison?","image":"","options":["P – Time series analysis; Q – Inter-firm comparison; R – Comparison with standards","P – Comparison with standards; Q – Cross-sectional analysis; R – Intra-firm comparison","P – Intra-firm comparison; Q – Comparison with standards; R – Cross-sectional analysis","P – Cross-sectional analysis; Q – Comparison with standards; R – Time series analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"P: Comparing with another enterprise is inter-firm comparison, also called cross-sectional analysis.\nQ: Comparing with a benchmark norm such as 1:1 is comparison with standards (industry expectations).\nR: Comparing the firm's own figures across years is intra-firm comparison, also called time series analysis.\nOnly option (4) matches all three correctly.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a firm is 2.5 : 1 and its working capital is Rs. 1,20,000. Calculate the amount of current assets.","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 1,44,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current ratio = 2.5 : 1 and working capital = Rs. 1,20,000.\nLet current liabilities = x, so current assets = 2.5x.\nWorking capital = 2.5x − x = 1.5x = 1,20,000, so x = Rs. 80,000.\nCurrent assets = 2.5 × 80,000 = Rs. 2,00,000.\nRs. 80,000 is the figure for current liabilities, not current assets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A trader keeps an average inventory of Rs. 60,000 and his inventory turnover ratio is 5 times. He sells goods at 25% above cost. Calculate his gross profit.","image":"","options":["Rs. 75,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 3,75,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"COGS = ITR × Average inventory = 5 × 60,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nSelling at 25% above cost means GP = 25% of COGS.\nGP = 25% × 3,00,000 = Rs. 75,000, and revenue = Rs. 3,75,000.\nTreating 25% as a margin on sales would wrongly give revenue of Rs. 4,00,000 and GP of Rs. 1,00,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the current ratio is 2 : 1, purchase of goods on credit reduces the current ratio.\nReason (R): An equal increase in the numerator and the denominator of a ratio greater than 1 lowers the ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A credit purchase increases inventory (a current asset) and trade payables (a current liability) by the same amount. For example, 50,000/25,000 becomes 60,000/35,000 = 1.71 : 1.\nR is true. Adding the same amount to both terms pulls a ratio that is above 1 down towards 1.\nR is exactly why the current ratio falls, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sona Ltd. reported a net profit after tax of Rs. 1,20,000 for 2023-24. The tax rate is 25%. Its only long-term debt is 12% Debentures of Rs. 5,00,000, outstanding for the whole year. A lender wants to judge how safely the company can pay its debenture interest. What is the interest coverage ratio of Sona Ltd.?","image":"","options":["2 times","2.67 times","3 times","3.67 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: profit after tax (PAT) = Rs. 1,20,000, tax rate = 25%, 12% debentures = Rs. 5,00,000.\nProfit before tax = PAT × 100/(100 − 25) = 1,20,000 × 100/75 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nInterest = 12% of Rs. 5,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nProfit before interest and tax = 1,60,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 2,20,000.\nInterest coverage ratio = 2,20,000 / 60,000 = 3.67 times.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has a current ratio of 3 : 1 and a quick ratio of 1.8 : 1. Inventory of ₹ 48,000 is the only difference between its current assets and quick assets. What is the firm's working capital?","image":"","options":["₹ 1,20,000","₹ 80,000","₹ 40,000","₹ 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let current liabilities = x. Then current assets = 3x and quick assets = 1.8x.\nInventory = current assets − quick assets: 48,000 = 3x − 1.8x = 1.2x, so x = ₹ 40,000.\nCurrent assets = 3 × 40,000 = ₹ 1,20,000.\nWorking capital = current assets − current liabilities = 1,20,000 − 40,000 = ₹ 80,000.\nOption (1) is current assets, (3) is current liabilities and (4) is quick assets, not working capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of ratio analysis?","image":"","options":["It ignores price-level changes","It ignores qualitative or non-monetary aspects","It simplifies complex accounting figures and brings out their relationships","Different firms follow different accounting practices"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Options (1), (2) and (4) are limitations listed in NCERT. They are price-level changes being ignored, qualitative aspects being ignored and variations in accounting practices.\nSimplifying complex figures and establishing relationships is listed as an ADVANTAGE of ratio analysis, not a limitation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"While computing the quick (acid-test) ratio, which of the following current assets is retained as a quick asset?","image":"","options":["Inventories","Prepaid expenses","Advance tax","Trade receivables"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Quick assets are current assets that can be quickly converted into cash.\nInventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax are removed from current assets, because they cannot readily be turned into cash.\nTrade receivables (debtors and bills receivable) are realisable in cash in the short run, so they stay in quick assets.\nInventory is the most tempting wrong option, but it is generally treated as the least liquid current asset.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the current ratio of Arun Ltd. from the following information:\nTotal assets Rs. 8,00,000; Shareholders' funds Rs. 4,50,000; Non-current liabilities Rs. 2,00,000; Fixed assets Rs. 5,20,000; Non-current investments Rs. 1,00,000.","image":"","options":["0.83 : 1","1.2 : 1","1.5 : 1","2 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets = Rs. 8,00,000 and non-current assets = 5,20,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 6,20,000.\nCurrent assets = 8,00,000 − 6,20,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nTotal assets also equal equity and liabilities, so current liabilities = 8,00,000 − (4,50,000 + 2,00,000) = Rs. 1,50,000.\nCurrent ratio = 1,80,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = 1.2 : 1. The option 0.83 : 1 is the inverse of the correct ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Category under functional classification):\nList-I\n(A) Quick ratio\n(B) Total assets to debt ratio\n(C) Trade payables turnover ratio\n(D) Book value per share\nList-II\n(i) Activity ratio\n(ii) Profitability ratio\n(iii) Liquidity ratio\n(iv) Solvency ratio","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Quick ratio measures short-term ability to pay, so it is a liquidity ratio: (iii).\n(B) Total assets to debt ratio measures how well long-term debt is covered by assets, so it is a solvency ratio: (iv).\n(C) Trade payables turnover shows how fast trade payables are turned over, so it is an activity ratio: (i).\n(D) Book value per share is listed by NCERT among profitability ratios: (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. has current assets of ₹60,000 and current liabilities of ₹30,000, giving a current ratio of 2 : 1. During the week it completes two transactions:\n(i) It pays trade creditors ₹10,000 by cheque.\n(ii) It sells an old machine with a book value of ₹8,000 for ₹6,000 in cash.\nWhat is the current ratio after both transactions?","image":"","options":["2.9 : 1","2.5 : 1","2.8 : 1","1.87 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Current assets (CA) = ₹60,000 and current liabilities (CL) = ₹30,000.\nEffect of (i): Paying creditors reduces both cash and creditors by ₹10,000. CA becomes ₹50,000 and CL becomes ₹20,000.\nEffect of (ii): The machine is a fixed asset, so only the cash received (₹6,000) is added to CA. The book value of ₹8,000 is irrelevant here. CA becomes ₹56,000 and CL stays at ₹20,000.\nNew current ratio = ₹56,000 / ₹20,000 = 2.8 : 1.\nThe distractor 2.9 : 1 wrongly adds the book value (₹8,000) instead of the cash received.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Credit revenue from operations of Neel Ltd. for the year was Rs. 7,20,000. Its average collection period was 45 days, taking a year of 360 days. Trade receivables at the beginning of the year were Rs. 70,000. What was the amount of trade receivables at the end of the year?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,10,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Collection period = 45 days and the year has 360 days.\nTR Turnover = 360 / 45 = 8 times.\nAverage TR = Credit revenue / Turnover = 7,20,000 / 8 = Rs. 90,000.\nOpening + Closing = 2 × 90,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nSo Closing TR = 1,80,000 − 70,000 = Rs. 1,10,000. The figure Rs. 90,000 is only the average.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a company is 2 : 1.\nStatement I: Purchase of goods on credit will reduce the current ratio.\nStatement II: Payment of Rs. 10,000 to creditors by cheque will reduce the current ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assume current assets are Rs. 50,000 and current liabilities are Rs. 25,000, giving 2 : 1.\nStatement I: credit purchase of Rs. 10,000 gives 60,000/35,000 = 1.71 : 1. The ratio falls, so the statement is correct.\nStatement II: payment of Rs. 10,000 gives 40,000/15,000 = 2.67 : 1. The ratio improves, so the statement is incorrect.\nWhen the ratio is above 1, an equal change in both items moves the ratio in the same direction as the change.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Ratio) with List-II (Category under functional classification):\nList-I\nA. Interest coverage ratio\nB. Working capital turnover ratio\nC. Acid-test ratio\nD. Price earning ratio\nList-II\n(i) Liquidity ratio\n(ii) Profitability ratio\n(iii) Solvency ratio\n(iv) Activity ratio","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Interest coverage measures the security of interest on long-term debt, so it is a solvency ratio.\nB-(iv): Working capital turnover measures how efficiently resources are used, so it is an activity ratio.\nC-(i): The acid-test (quick) ratio measures short-term solvency, so it is a liquidity ratio.\nD-(ii): The P/E ratio is listed by NCERT among the profitability ratios.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"A ratio can be expressed in several forms. Which of the following is NOT one of the forms of expressing a ratio mentioned in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Fraction or proportion","Percentage","Number of times","Absolute rupee amount"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT says a ratio may be expressed as a fraction, a proportion, a percentage or a number of times.\nFor example, the gross profit ratio is shown as a percentage, and the inventory turnover ratio is shown as a number of times (such as 6 times).\nA ratio is a relationship between two numbers, so it has no rupee unit. An absolute rupee amount is therefore not a ratio.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Rekha Ltd., calculate the book value per share:\nEquity share capital (60,000 shares of ₹10 each): ₹6,00,000\n9% Preference share capital: ₹2,00,000\nReserves and surplus: ₹1,80,000","image":"","options":["₹16.33","₹10.00","₹13.00","₹3.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Shareholders' funds = 6,00,000 + 2,00,000 + 1,80,000 = ₹9,80,000\nEquity shareholders' funds = Shareholders' funds − Preference share capital = 9,80,000 − 2,00,000 = ₹7,80,000\nFormula: Book value per share = Equity shareholders' funds / Number of equity shares\nSubstituting: 7,80,000 / 60,000 = ₹13.00\n₹16.33 is wrong because it wrongly includes preference capital.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the Interest Coverage Ratio:\nNet profit after tax: Rs. 1,20,000\nTax rate: 40%\n12% Debentures: Rs. 5,00,000","image":"","options":["4.33 times","3.33 times","2 times","2.4 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Profit after tax (PAT) = Rs. 1,20,000, tax rate = 40%, 12% debentures = Rs. 5,00,000. \nProfit before tax = 1,20,000 × 100/60 = Rs. 2,00,000. \nInterest = 12% of 5,00,000 = Rs. 60,000. \nProfit before interest and tax = 2,00,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 2,60,000. \nInterest Coverage Ratio = 2,60,000 / 60,000 = 4.33 times. \nOption (2) is tempting but wrong: it uses profit before tax without adding back interest. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the Net Profit Ratio:\nRevenue from Operations: Rs. 6,00,000\nCost of Revenue from Operations: Rs. 4,20,000\nNet Profit after Tax: Rs. 72,000","image":"","options":["12%","30%","17.14%","70%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from Operations = Rs. 6,00,000; Net Profit (PAT) = Rs. 72,000.\nFormula: Net Profit Ratio = Net Profit / Revenue from Operations × 100. NCERT treats net profit as profit after tax.\nSubstituting: 72,000 / 6,00,000 × 100 = 12%.\nThe cost of revenue figure is not needed. 30% is the gross profit ratio, (6,00,000 − 4,20,000) / 6,00,000 × 100, not the net profit ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":159,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are included in current assets while calculating the current ratio?\nAdvance tax, Prepaid expenses, Current investments, Short-term loans and advances, Bills payable, Long-term loans and advances, Accrued income","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Current assets: advance tax, prepaid expenses, current investments, short-term loans and advances, and accrued income. That makes 5 items. \nBills payable is not a current asset. It is a trade payable, which is a current liability. \nLong-term loans and advances are not current assets. They are non-current assets. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":160,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following ratios:\nInventory turnover ratio, Current ratio, Gross profit ratio, Trade receivables turnover ratio, Debt-equity ratio, Working capital turnover ratio, Operating ratio.\nHow many of these are composite ratios under the traditional classification?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inventory turnover = cost of revenue from operations (P&L) ÷ average inventory (Balance Sheet). This is composite.\nCurrent ratio and debt-equity ratio use only Balance Sheet figures, so they are Balance Sheet ratios.\nGross profit ratio and operating ratio use only P&L figures, so they are P&L ratios.\nTrade receivables turnover = credit revenue (P&L) ÷ trade receivables (Balance Sheet). This is composite.\nWorking capital turnover = revenue (P&L) ÷ working capital (Balance Sheet). This is composite.\nTotal composite ratios = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":161,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The debt-equity ratio of a company is 0.5 : 1. The company collects Rs. 50,000 in cash from its trade receivables. What is the effect on the debt-equity ratio?","image":"","options":["It will increase","It will decrease","It will not change","It will first increase and then decrease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Debt-equity ratio = Long-term debts / Shareholders' funds.\nCollecting cash from debtors only changes the make-up of current assets: trade receivables fall and cash rises.\nNeither long-term debt nor shareholders' funds is affected, so the ratio does not change.\nThe tempting answer 'decrease' is wrong because no debt is repaid in this transaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":162,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"While calculating the Trade Receivables Turnover Ratio, how should debtors be taken?","image":"","options":["Before deducting the provision for doubtful debts","After deducting the provision for doubtful debts","After excluding bills receivable","Only at the closing balance, even when the opening balance is available"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: debtors are taken before any provision for doubtful debts is deducted.\nTrade receivables include both debtors and bills receivable, so excluding bills receivable is wrong.\nThe denominator is average trade receivables, i.e. (opening + closing)/2. Closing figures alone are used only when opening figures are not available.\nThe tempting option (2) fails because the provision is not deducted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":163,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of Vansh Ltd. is 2.5 : 1. Its current assets are Rs. 75,000 and current liabilities are Rs. 30,000. By how much must the current assets decline, with current liabilities unchanged, to bring the current ratio to 2 : 1?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 7,500","Rs. 45,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: CA = Rs. 75,000, CL = Rs. 30,000, target ratio = 2 : 1.\nFormula: Required CA = Target ratio × CL.\nSubstituting: Required CA = 2 × 30,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nDecline needed = 75,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-164","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":164,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information, calculate the current ratio:\nTotal assets Rs. 6,00,000\nFixed assets Rs. 3,50,000\nNon-current investments Rs. 1,00,000\nShareholders' funds Rs. 3,80,000\nNon-current liabilities Rs. 1,20,000","image":"","options":["1.25 : 1","1.5 : 1","0.75 : 1","2.5 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets Rs. 6,00,000. Non-current assets = 3,50,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nCurrent assets = 6,00,000 − 4,50,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nTotal assets = Shareholders' funds + Non-current liabilities + Current liabilities.\nSo current liabilities = 6,00,000 − (3,80,000 + 1,20,000) = Rs. 1,00,000.\nCurrent ratio = 1,50,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = 1.5 : 1.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-165","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":165,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The balance sheet of Arjun Ltd. shows the following:\nEquity share capital Rs. 8,00,000\nReserves and surplus Rs. 2,00,000\n12% Debentures Rs. 5,00,000\nCurrent liabilities Rs. 3,00,000\nFixed assets Rs. 12,00,000\nCurrent assets Rs. 6,00,000\nRevenue from operations for the year is Rs. 36,00,000. Calculate the Net Assets (Capital Employed) Turnover Ratio.","image":"","options":["2.4 times","2 times","3 times","3.6 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Shareholders' funds = 8,00,000 + 2,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000. Long-term debt = Rs. 5,00,000.\nCapital employed = 10,00,000 + 5,00,000 = Rs. 15,00,000.\nCheck: total assets 18,00,000 − CL 3,00,000 = Rs. 15,00,000.\nSubstituting: 36,00,000 / 15,00,000 = 2.4 times.\nUsing total assets gives 2 (wrong). Using fixed assets gives 3, which is the fixed assets turnover (wrong).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-166","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":166,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The current ratio of a company is 2 : 1. How many of the following transactions would IMPROVE (increase) this ratio?\n(a) Payment to creditors by cheque\n(b) Purchase of goods on credit\n(c) Sale of an old machine (book value Rs. 8,000) for Rs. 6,000 in cash\n(d) Sale of goods costing Rs. 20,000 for Rs. 24,000 in cash\n(e) Cash collected from debtors","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Improves. Current assets (CA) and current liabilities (CL) fall by the same amount. When the ratio is above 1, an equal fall raises it. \n(b) Reduces. CA and CL rise by the same amount, which pulls a ratio above 1 down. \n(c) Improves. Cash (a current asset) rises by Rs. 6,000 while CL stays the same. The loss on sale does not matter here. \n(d) Improves. Inventory falls by Rs. 20,000 and cash rises by Rs. 24,000, so CA increases by Rs. 4,000. \n(e) No change. One current asset is simply replaced by another. \nSo 3 transactions improve the ratio. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-167","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":167,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Trade receivables turnover ratio is a composite ratio.\nReason (R): Trade receivables turnover ratio is an activity ratio because it shows how many times receivables are converted into cash in an accounting period.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Credit revenue from operations comes from the P&L and trade receivables come from the Balance Sheet. A ratio drawing on both statements is composite.\nR is true. The ratio measures the speed of collection, which makes it an activity (efficiency) ratio.\nHowever, R uses the functional basis (purpose), while A rests on the traditional basis (source statements). So R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-168","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":168,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The quick ratio is considered a better measure of the liquidity position of a business than the current ratio.\nReason (R): The quick ratio leaves out non-liquid current assets such as inventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the quick ratio is a better measure of liquidity than the current ratio. It acts as a supplementary check on liquidity, which is why it is also called the acid-test ratio.\nR is true. Inventories, prepaid expenses and advance tax are excluded while calculating quick assets.\nR explains A. The ratio is a stricter test of liquidity precisely because these non-liquid items are removed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-169","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":169,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Current liabilities of Sunrise Ltd. are Rs. 60,000. Its current ratio is 2.5 : 1 and its liquid ratio is 1.2 : 1. Assuming inventory is the only non-liquid current asset, find the value of inventory.","image":"","options":["Rs. 78,000","Rs. 72,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 1,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Current liabilities = Rs. 60,000; Current ratio = 2.5 : 1; Liquid ratio = 1.2 : 1.\nCurrent assets = 2.5 × 60,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nLiquid assets = 1.2 × 60,000 = Rs. 72,000.\nInventory = Current assets − Liquid assets = 1,50,000 − 72,000 = Rs. 78,000.\nThe option Rs. 72,000 is the liquid assets, not the inventory.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-170","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":170,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Leela Traders reports revenue from operations of Rs. 6,00,000 and a gross profit ratio of 25% (on revenue from operations). Its average inventory is Rs. 75,000. Calculate the inventory turnover ratio.","image":"","options":["8 times","6.4 times","6 times","2 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 6,00,000; GP = 25% of revenue; Average inventory = Rs. 75,000.\nGross profit = 25% × 6,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nCost of revenue from operations = 6,00,000 − 1,50,000 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nITR = 4,50,000 ÷ 75,000 = 6 times.\nThe 8 times option wrongly uses revenue instead of cost of revenue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-171","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":171,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about a company whose debt-equity ratio is 0.5 : 1.\nStatement I: Redemption of debentures in cash will decrease the debt-equity ratio.\nStatement II: Further issue of equity shares for cash will increase the debt-equity ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Redeeming debentures lowers long-term debt while equity stays the same. For example, 4,00,000/10,00,000 = 0.4 : 1, down from 5,00,000/10,00,000.\nStatement II is incorrect. Issuing equity shares raises shareholders' funds, which is the denominator, so the ratio decreases. For example, 5,00,000/11,00,000 = 0.45 : 1.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-172","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":172,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A higher interest coverage ratio indicates greater safety of interest payable to long-term lenders.\nStatement II: Interest coverage ratio is a liquidity ratio because it measures the firm's ability to pay its short-term obligations.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. ICR shows how many times the interest on long-term debts is covered by the profits available for interest. A higher cover means interest payments are safer.\nStatement II is false. ICR is one of the solvency ratios listed by NCERT. It relates to the servicing of long-term debt, not to short-term obligations.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-RATIOS-173","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting Ratios","qno":173,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","type":"mcq","question":"The quick ratio of Sanjay Ltd. is 1.5 : 1. Which one of the following transactions will increase this ratio?","image":"","options":["Purchase of inventory for cash","Collection of cash from trade receivables","Payment of Rs. 20,000 to trade payables","Purchase of goods on credit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Assume quick assets are Rs. 1,50,000 and current liabilities are Rs. 1,00,000.\n(1) Cash falls but inventory is not a quick asset, so quick assets fall and the ratio decreases.\n(2) Cash rises and receivables fall by the same amount, so quick assets are unchanged and there is no change.\n(3) Quick assets and current liabilities both fall by Rs. 20,000, giving 1,30,000 ÷ 80,000 = 1.625. Because the ratio was above 1, it improves.\n(4) Current liabilities rise while quick assets are unchanged, so the ratio decreases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting Ratios","difficulty":"medium"}]});
